1
00:00:00,891 --> 00:00:03,361
(We All Need a Day Like Today)

2
00:00:03,361 --> 00:00:05,671
- Nice to see you, everyone. - My gosh.

3
00:00:05,671 --> 00:00:07,941
Woo Il. Lee Jung. It's been a while, you two.

4
00:00:07,941 --> 00:00:09,641
- Gosh, long time no see. - Hello.

5
00:00:09,641 --> 00:00:11,411
- Long time no see. - Long time no see.

6
00:00:11,411 --> 00:00:12,711
- It's nice to see you. - Where have you been?

7
00:00:12,711 --> 00:00:14,911
- I wonder what brings him here. - Wait, Woo Il.

8
00:00:14,911 --> 00:00:16,211
How did you end up getting so tanned?

9
00:00:16,211 --> 00:00:18,651
I was abroad for two weeks.

10
00:00:18,911 --> 00:00:19,951
I went to Southeast Asia.

11
00:00:19,951 --> 00:00:21,581
I went to Indonesia and the Philippines.

12
00:00:21,581 --> 00:00:23,351
We talked about you all the time.

13
00:00:23,351 --> 00:00:24,381
- Really? - Yes.

14
00:00:24,381 --> 00:00:25,691
- You should join us. - We missed you.

15
00:00:25,691 --> 00:00:26,691
- You got us hooked. - Right.

16
00:00:26,691 --> 00:00:28,221
I'm sure he came to see Lee Jung today.

17
00:00:28,221 --> 00:00:29,221
- Right. - Yes.

18
00:00:29,221 --> 00:00:30,721
- He's a fan of her show. - Right.

19
00:00:30,721 --> 00:00:32,891
I really wanted to go to her concert.

20
00:00:33,091 --> 00:00:34,731
- How about the one in November? - In November?

21
00:00:34,731 --> 00:00:35,731
- Yes. - Sure.

22
00:00:35,731 --> 00:00:36,831
You must go no matter what.

23
00:00:36,831 --> 00:00:38,731
- Did you pass the driving test? - What happened with that?

24
00:00:38,731 --> 00:00:39,801
Right, what happened?

25
00:00:39,971 --> 00:00:43,541
(Lee Jung failed the driving test.)

26
00:00:44,671 --> 00:00:46,141
I'll try...

27
00:00:46,141 --> 00:00:47,611
- to pass the test this year. - Okay.

28
00:00:47,611 --> 00:00:49,081
I'll give you all a ride.

29
00:00:49,511 --> 00:00:51,981
- I think I'm good. - You...

30
00:00:51,981 --> 00:00:53,251
You can give Woo Il a ride.

31
00:00:53,251 --> 00:00:55,121
(She'll give her true fan, Woo Il, a ride.)

32
00:00:56,321 --> 00:00:57,581
(Retouch)

33
00:00:57,581 --> 00:00:58,651
"Retouch."

34
00:01:00,321 --> 00:01:01,661
(The sound of brushstrokes fills Kian84's studio.)

35
00:01:01,661 --> 00:01:02,991
It's his studio.

36
00:01:03,261 --> 00:01:05,231
I could have done this in one go.

37
00:01:07,091 --> 00:01:09,631
(Kian84 is working on something.)

38
00:01:10,831 --> 00:01:11,831
(Amazed)

39
00:01:11,831 --> 00:01:13,031
That's huge.

40
00:01:14,071 --> 00:01:15,341
What's the size number for this?

41
00:01:15,401 --> 00:01:16,901
The size number is 120.

42
00:01:19,141 --> 00:01:20,171
Darn it.

43
00:01:25,951 --> 00:01:27,651
But why do you look so unhappy?

44
00:01:28,481 --> 00:01:30,481
Darn it. This is so annoying.

45
00:01:30,481 --> 00:01:31,721
Why are you cursing?

46
00:01:34,151 --> 00:01:37,321
I've been trying to work on my art.

47
00:01:38,031 --> 00:01:39,591
But trying to work on my art...

48
00:01:39,591 --> 00:01:42,131
while starring on TV shows and running...

49
00:01:43,131 --> 00:01:44,671
might have weakened my concentration.

50
00:01:44,671 --> 00:01:46,731
Or I just might be struggling to channel myself into my work.

51
00:01:46,731 --> 00:01:48,901
- But it's true that I'm struggling. - Really?

52
00:01:49,741 --> 00:01:52,741
(His passion has been driving him for the past few years.)

53
00:01:54,441 --> 00:01:55,581
(He managed to win the Grand Award...)

54
00:01:55,581 --> 00:01:56,641
(while pulling off a busy schedule.)

55
00:01:56,641 --> 00:01:59,281
- Right, you're so busy. - Congratulations.

56
00:02:00,481 --> 00:02:03,681
(He successfully completed the New York City Marathon.)

57
00:02:05,451 --> 00:02:09,821
(In no time, running has been an important part of his life.)

58
00:02:09,821 --> 00:02:13,431
I ran about 240km just in September.

59
00:02:14,261 --> 00:02:17,361
- For 1 month? - Yes. I ran about 230 to 240km.

60
00:02:17,361 --> 00:02:19,531
- He ran a lot. - He ran more distance than a car.

61
00:02:19,701 --> 00:02:20,701
That's true.

62
00:02:20,701 --> 00:02:22,871
Then you ran at least 8km every day.

63
00:02:23,001 --> 00:02:26,511
Running improves your health, but makes you age fast.

64
00:02:26,871 --> 00:02:28,141
- It makes you age? - Yes.

65
00:02:28,141 --> 00:02:29,681
- It ages you? - It quickens...

66
00:02:29,681 --> 00:02:30,811
- the aging process. - He does seem older.

67
00:02:30,811 --> 00:02:32,781
Isn't it because you're running way too much?

68
00:02:32,781 --> 00:02:34,251
- Maybe it's excessive. - Yes, cut down on running.

69
00:02:34,251 --> 00:02:35,251
- Here's the thing. - You can cut down on running.

70
00:02:35,251 --> 00:02:36,581
I'm not a good runner.

71
00:02:36,581 --> 00:02:37,951
Because I want to improve, I end up running a lot.

72
00:02:37,951 --> 00:02:40,191
Good runners run a lot because they want to stay that way.

73
00:02:40,421 --> 00:02:41,921
That's how we're all turning into Sean.

74
00:02:43,191 --> 00:02:44,991
- Let's say you keep running... - If you stop running...

75
00:02:44,991 --> 00:02:46,331
- and you improve. - Right.

76
00:02:46,331 --> 00:02:47,731
Then the distance you used to run...

77
00:02:47,731 --> 00:02:49,131
- won't satisfy you. - Right.

78
00:02:49,261 --> 00:02:50,731
- Yes. - You'll have to run a little more.

79
00:02:50,731 --> 00:02:52,601
When runners take a break, their endurance declines a bit.

80
00:02:53,701 --> 00:02:56,701
I work on my art at least three days out of the week.

81
00:02:56,701 --> 00:02:57,941
Do you have time for that?

82
00:02:58,171 --> 00:03:01,041
Well, I like some of my paintings,

83
00:03:01,041 --> 00:03:02,541
not all of them.

84
00:03:05,111 --> 00:03:08,051
I feel like I'm in a slump.

85
00:03:10,321 --> 00:03:13,951
(Out of frustration, he tries to change it around.)

86
00:03:15,391 --> 00:03:17,991
(Does it look good?)

87
00:03:18,931 --> 00:03:19,991
Darn.

88
00:03:21,261 --> 00:03:23,431
(He goes back to brushing.)

89
00:03:23,431 --> 00:03:25,301
You know what I noticed about him?

90
00:03:26,431 --> 00:03:28,141
He has such high standards for his work.

91
00:03:28,141 --> 00:03:29,241
The standards keep going up.

92
00:03:29,241 --> 00:03:30,901
I drew cartoons for a long time.

93
00:03:31,511 --> 00:03:34,011
But I've been painting only for 3 to 4 years.

94
00:03:34,471 --> 00:03:36,411
So I'm still painting like a novice.

95
00:03:38,481 --> 00:03:40,481
(Let me think.)

96
00:03:41,051 --> 00:03:42,981
That looks pretty good.

97
00:03:45,051 --> 00:03:46,921
- You're blurring the lines. - Yes.

98
00:03:48,391 --> 00:03:50,191
I guess you painters are on the same page.

99
00:03:50,191 --> 00:03:52,191
That technique is called sfukato.

100
00:03:52,191 --> 00:03:53,461
- What? What was that? - It's to blur the lines.

101
00:03:53,461 --> 00:03:54,901
- Sfukato... - Gosh, this is so annoying.

102
00:03:54,901 --> 00:03:57,461
(Gosh, this is so annoying.)

103
00:03:57,461 --> 00:03:59,771
- I guess you're right. - Yes. Of course.

104
00:03:59,771 --> 00:04:01,741
When you look at Mona Lisa,

105
00:04:01,741 --> 00:04:03,941
- the lines are blurred. - You're bringing that up?

106
00:04:04,341 --> 00:04:05,971
We use the technique to blur the lines.

107
00:04:05,971 --> 00:04:07,811
- It's to soften the lines. - Yes, to soften them.

108
00:04:07,811 --> 00:04:10,141
- You can click as a fellow painter. - Of course. It's profound.

109
00:04:10,911 --> 00:04:12,411
- I knew it. - You should learn from me.

110
00:04:12,911 --> 00:04:14,821
- He's the best painter. - I'll stop painting now.

111
00:04:15,121 --> 00:04:16,151
What?

112
00:04:16,151 --> 00:04:18,191
You gave high praise for his work in Illo.

113
00:04:18,721 --> 00:04:20,751
(Gosh, your drawing has improved a lot.)

114
00:04:21,321 --> 00:04:23,261
(He recently spoke highly of Moosquiat's work.)

115
00:04:23,521 --> 00:04:25,231
- I was just saying that. - Really?

116
00:04:25,231 --> 00:04:27,791
- Was it an empty compliment? - It was good. But...

117
00:04:27,791 --> 00:04:29,101
You're annoyed that...

118
00:04:29,101 --> 00:04:30,331
- he's compared to you, right? - This is annoying.

119
00:04:31,161 --> 00:04:33,131
I wasn't treated like this when I drew webtoons.

120
00:04:33,301 --> 00:04:34,501
Let yourself be...

121
00:04:34,501 --> 00:04:35,701
- motivated like this. - Okay, Hyun Moo.

122
00:04:36,241 --> 00:04:38,771
(Kian84 keeps reapplying the paint.)

123
00:04:39,171 --> 00:04:40,671
(Groaning)

124
00:04:41,581 --> 00:04:42,911
Gosh, this is exhausting.

125
00:04:44,641 --> 00:04:45,751
This isn't working out.

126
00:04:45,751 --> 00:04:48,051
(Complaining)

127
00:04:50,551 --> 00:04:51,651
Darn it.

128
00:04:52,791 --> 00:04:54,221
What don't you like about it?

129
00:04:54,291 --> 00:04:56,891
The thing is, acrylic paints don't mix well.

130
00:04:57,261 --> 00:04:58,331
They dry up fast.

131
00:05:00,791 --> 00:05:03,201
(Come on, just blend already.)

132
00:05:05,171 --> 00:05:06,371
(Thud)

133
00:05:06,371 --> 00:05:08,771
Darn it. The chair...

134
00:05:09,641 --> 00:05:11,941
- Why are you so angry? - Why do you sound so stressed?

135
00:05:11,941 --> 00:05:13,271
- So... - The chair did nothing wrong.

136
00:05:13,271 --> 00:05:14,911
when I go home after painting,

137
00:05:14,911 --> 00:05:16,611
- I'm in a foul mood. - Well...

138
00:05:16,611 --> 00:05:18,111
I'm so happy when I paint.

139
00:05:18,511 --> 00:05:20,011
I used to giggle when I drew cartoons.

140
00:05:20,181 --> 00:05:21,511
I used to giggle...

141
00:05:21,511 --> 00:05:22,851
- while working alone. - Right.

142
00:05:23,051 --> 00:05:24,851
- But art is different. - When something doesn't go right,

143
00:05:24,851 --> 00:05:25,851
nothing goes right.

144
00:05:25,851 --> 00:05:26,891
That's so true.

145
00:05:26,891 --> 00:05:29,361
Lee Jung, do you also curse when you can't execute some moves?

146
00:05:29,691 --> 00:05:31,421
- I do that a lot. - Really?

147
00:05:31,691 --> 00:05:33,161
That defines...

148
00:05:33,161 --> 00:05:34,591
- if it's your hobby or career. - Right.

149
00:05:34,591 --> 00:05:35,931
- We get serious if it's our career. - It's not my career.

150
00:05:36,031 --> 00:05:37,531
- Right. That's true. - Of course.

151
00:05:37,531 --> 00:05:39,971
- Kian84 has a goal. - It's fun when it's just a hobby.

152
00:05:40,071 --> 00:05:42,971
I always want to do better than what I'm capable of.

153
00:05:43,471 --> 00:05:44,741
- I guess you're not satisfied. - No.

154
00:05:44,741 --> 00:05:45,741
(It's not easy to satisfy himself.)

155
00:05:46,371 --> 00:05:48,211
(He grabs a working chair.)

156
00:05:49,481 --> 00:05:52,411
(Then he focuses on brushing once again.)

157
00:05:55,621 --> 00:05:57,621
(Groaning)

158
00:05:57,621 --> 00:05:59,421
Come on, I can't seem to get this right.

159
00:06:01,961 --> 00:06:04,691
(He dislikes his painting even more than usual.)

160
00:06:04,691 --> 00:06:05,691
Darn it.

161
00:06:06,831 --> 00:06:09,901
(Darn it, I'll stop here.)

162
00:06:13,801 --> 00:06:15,041
- That looks great. - It's great.

163
00:06:15,341 --> 00:06:16,971
I couldn't execute it the way I envisioned it.

164
00:06:16,971 --> 00:06:18,101
Nothing ever works that way.

165
00:06:19,311 --> 00:06:21,041
(Grunting)

166
00:06:21,571 --> 00:06:24,241
(He grabs another canvas.)

167
00:06:27,451 --> 00:06:30,651
(He tosses the painting to the floor.)

168
00:06:32,851 --> 00:06:36,591
(He was inspired by Rembrandt.)

169
00:06:36,591 --> 00:06:38,531
- I'm working hard on this. - Wasn't it from earlier this year?

170
00:06:40,161 --> 00:06:41,561
I repainted it.

171
00:06:41,761 --> 00:06:43,631
Is this how artists think?

172
00:06:46,131 --> 00:06:49,471
(Kian84 brings out something else.)

173
00:06:51,671 --> 00:06:54,071
(Opening)

174
00:06:55,381 --> 00:06:56,411
(He applies the gesso before painting.)

175
00:06:56,411 --> 00:06:57,941
- That's gesso? - Gesso.

176
00:07:00,051 --> 00:07:02,621
What are you doing, Hee Min?

177
00:07:03,421 --> 00:07:05,421
You're doing a hideous job with your painting.

178
00:07:07,521 --> 00:07:09,661
(Glancing up)

179
00:07:10,961 --> 00:07:13,161
(What should I do?)

180
00:07:15,561 --> 00:07:17,731
(Startled)

181
00:07:17,731 --> 00:07:19,071
(Shocked)

182
00:07:19,531 --> 00:07:20,631
- Did you paint that over? - Why?

183
00:07:21,171 --> 00:07:22,301
- Did you paint that over? - Why?

184
00:07:22,801 --> 00:07:24,671
- That was great. Why? - Why did you do that?

185
00:07:25,111 --> 00:07:27,311
- What a pity. - Gosh, that's such a pity.

186
00:07:27,471 --> 00:07:29,441
I have a question. Why did you paint that over?

187
00:07:29,841 --> 00:07:32,151
- I should reuse the canvas. - Right, it'd be a waste.

188
00:07:32,151 --> 00:07:33,751
- Because it'd be a waste? - Not to waste the canvas?

189
00:07:33,751 --> 00:07:35,121
Partially, yes.

190
00:07:35,381 --> 00:07:37,221
When I don't like it, I just paint over it.

191
00:07:37,221 --> 00:07:38,491
- Painters do that? - Yes.

192
00:07:38,821 --> 00:07:42,121
Here's what I thought. So you didn't like that.

193
00:07:42,121 --> 00:07:43,761
You could've kept it as it was.

194
00:07:43,861 --> 00:07:45,631
You could have started with a new canvas.

195
00:07:45,791 --> 00:07:48,261
Once you get another inspiration, you can work on that again.

196
00:07:48,261 --> 00:07:50,331
All the time and effort would go to waste, otherwise.

197
00:07:50,661 --> 00:07:53,731
No. To do that, I was too frustrated with that.

198
00:07:53,731 --> 00:07:54,801
- It was that bad? - I see.

199
00:07:54,801 --> 00:07:56,441
I have a question then.

200
00:07:56,441 --> 00:07:58,071
How do you work on your art, Moosquiat?

201
00:07:58,071 --> 00:07:59,811
- Let's say I'm not satisfied. - Yes.

202
00:07:59,811 --> 00:08:00,871
I give it away as it is.

203
00:08:02,111 --> 00:08:03,481
"That's how you look."

204
00:08:03,781 --> 00:08:06,011
- You leave it as incomplete? - Even if it's not what you wanted?

205
00:08:06,011 --> 00:08:07,081
If it's not...

206
00:08:07,411 --> 00:08:08,851
"I hate it." Then I give it away.

207
00:08:09,321 --> 00:08:10,381
"Here you go."

208
00:08:11,351 --> 00:08:12,991
My gosh. Come on.

209
00:08:13,491 --> 00:08:15,961
How many months have I been working on this? It's all wet now.

210
00:08:16,761 --> 00:08:17,891
Hee Min.

211
00:08:19,131 --> 00:08:20,631
I didn't like that either.

212
00:08:20,761 --> 00:08:23,101
So I painted that over with gesso.

213
00:08:23,731 --> 00:08:26,671
It's been one and a half years since my last exhibit.

214
00:08:27,971 --> 00:08:29,141
I didn't like any of them.

215
00:08:29,601 --> 00:08:31,201
So you couldn't have another exhibit.

216
00:08:31,471 --> 00:08:34,571
I tried to paint clearly like animated cartoons.

217
00:08:34,941 --> 00:08:36,811
Then I tried utilizing gemstones.

218
00:08:36,811 --> 00:08:38,511
- They were beautiful. - I also tried doing oil paintings.

219
00:08:38,911 --> 00:08:41,111
Then I came back to working with acrylic paints.

220
00:08:41,511 --> 00:08:44,051
I'm not a good painter or anything.

221
00:08:45,051 --> 00:08:46,521
So I should be happy with whatever I have.

222
00:08:47,221 --> 00:08:51,391
But in my own way, I try my best to produce a good painting.

223
00:08:52,391 --> 00:08:53,861
Because there's no deadline,

224
00:08:54,631 --> 00:08:56,861
I keep changing my approaches.

225
00:08:57,501 --> 00:09:00,601
I try out a method. If I don't like it, I start over.

226
00:09:02,201 --> 00:09:03,501
So that's why I'm not so happy...

227
00:09:03,501 --> 00:09:05,311
when I paint these days. I'm quite stressed out.

228
00:09:08,041 --> 00:09:09,811
Right. You used to laugh a lot when you did cartoons.

229
00:09:10,381 --> 00:09:13,411
(He also taught people what he was good at.)

230
00:09:13,951 --> 00:09:14,951
(Painter84 used to depict romance.)

231
00:09:15,051 --> 00:09:17,351
- Gosh, he looks so young. - Yes, he does look young in this.

232
00:09:18,021 --> 00:09:19,751
(Working with paints is still unfamiliar,)

233
00:09:19,751 --> 00:09:21,521
(but he's been practicing it for the joy of it.)

234
00:09:22,491 --> 00:09:24,561
Right. You had so much fun back then.

235
00:09:24,561 --> 00:09:25,761
I forgot about that.

236
00:09:26,391 --> 00:09:27,431
Yes.

237
00:09:29,701 --> 00:09:31,261
- What a pity. - Right.

238
00:09:31,261 --> 00:09:32,271
(He spreads gesso over it with pursed lips.)

239
00:09:33,001 --> 00:09:34,071
Did it dry up?

240
00:09:34,071 --> 00:09:35,341
(He poured his heart into painting these waves.)

241
00:09:35,741 --> 00:09:38,201
(He spreads the gesso on top of them without hesitation.)

242
00:09:38,911 --> 00:09:40,641
I guess it's equivalent to potters breaking his works.

243
00:09:40,941 --> 00:09:42,611
What could I do, though?

244
00:09:42,611 --> 00:09:44,281
I can't display a painting I don't like at my exhibit.

245
00:09:44,711 --> 00:09:46,011
My gosh.

246
00:09:46,481 --> 00:09:48,551
I get what he's going through, but what a bummer.

247
00:09:51,121 --> 00:09:53,751
(His work of 7 months goes back to a blank canvas.)

248
00:09:53,751 --> 00:09:54,891
Seven months?

249
00:09:56,491 --> 00:09:58,491
- Do you see another white canvas? - Yes.

250
00:09:58,661 --> 00:10:01,661
I worked on that for four months. But I painted that over too.

251
00:10:01,901 --> 00:10:03,761
- What? This... - You did? My goodness.

252
00:10:06,071 --> 00:10:08,001
My gosh. No!

253
00:10:08,571 --> 00:10:09,671
Darn!

254
00:10:11,741 --> 00:10:12,971
No!

255
00:10:14,041 --> 00:10:15,881
(He accidentally makes a big mistake.)

256
00:10:16,241 --> 00:10:18,811
- Oh, no. What's he going to do? - My gosh.

257
00:10:18,811 --> 00:10:20,111
Oh, how tiresome that is.

258
00:10:21,111 --> 00:10:22,221
Darn!

259
00:10:23,021 --> 00:10:26,451
(Spilling over)

260
00:10:27,891 --> 00:10:28,921
(Speechless)

261
00:10:28,921 --> 00:10:30,891
Why do all the bad things happen...

262
00:10:30,891 --> 00:10:32,461
- in one day? - Right.

263
00:10:33,591 --> 00:10:35,331
There's a day like that. Seriously.

264
00:10:35,331 --> 00:10:36,801
This is driving me crazy. Come on.

265
00:10:38,061 --> 00:10:41,001
(Nothing is going my way today.)

266
00:10:42,871 --> 00:10:43,871
(Disgruntled)

267
00:10:47,911 --> 00:10:49,441
(He tries to save whatever he can.)

268
00:10:49,441 --> 00:10:51,511
Oh, no. The carpet is ruined.

269
00:10:53,311 --> 00:10:54,511
Come on.

270
00:10:55,981 --> 00:10:57,151
What's wrong with me?

271
00:10:59,021 --> 00:11:01,021
- You're most upset with yourself. - Right.

272
00:11:01,021 --> 00:11:02,821
- You'd be so annoyed. - You can't blame anyone else.

273
00:11:02,821 --> 00:11:03,921
- Right. - Come on.

274
00:11:04,491 --> 00:11:05,931
What a way to pile it on.

275
00:11:07,391 --> 00:11:09,801
(He removes the remaining gesso on the canvas.)

276
00:11:12,501 --> 00:11:15,541
(The carpet, his hands, and clothes are all stained.)

277
00:11:16,071 --> 00:11:20,211
(What a royal mess.)

278
00:11:23,811 --> 00:11:25,951
You sweated a lot.

279
00:11:26,811 --> 00:11:29,551
(He changes into his regular clothes from his work clothes.)

280
00:11:29,551 --> 00:11:31,381
(Grabbing)

281
00:11:32,051 --> 00:11:33,891
- He's leaving. - Right. When it's not working,

282
00:11:33,891 --> 00:11:36,121
- you must do something else. - Right, you should stop.

283
00:11:36,291 --> 00:11:38,261
Ae Ong. Meow.

284
00:11:40,261 --> 00:11:42,301
- We rarely get to see Ae Ong. - Right.

285
00:11:42,931 --> 00:11:44,031
I'm leaving.

286
00:11:45,501 --> 00:11:46,971
(Coy)

287
00:11:48,971 --> 00:11:53,071
(Kian84 leaves after working for hours.)

288
00:11:58,241 --> 00:11:59,281
Hello.

289
00:11:59,281 --> 00:12:00,851
Hello.

290
00:12:03,151 --> 00:12:04,981
(Kian84 is heading somewhere in a taxi.)

291
00:12:04,981 --> 00:12:06,091
Where is he going?

292
00:12:09,421 --> 00:12:10,761
Are you suddenly done with your day?

293
00:12:10,761 --> 00:12:11,861
Or to refresh?

294
00:12:12,591 --> 00:12:13,691
Thank you.

295
00:12:14,361 --> 00:12:15,731
How will he refresh himself?

296
00:12:16,001 --> 00:12:18,001
- I really hope it's not running. - Stop running.

297
00:12:18,231 --> 00:12:19,971
Judging by his outfit, he's not going to run.

298
00:12:20,501 --> 00:12:22,971
- What? Is he going on a trip? - What? He's going out of Seoul.

299
00:12:23,671 --> 00:12:25,741
You can buy art supplies at the Express Bus Terminal.

300
00:12:26,211 --> 00:12:27,311
- Right. - My gosh.

301
00:12:27,311 --> 00:12:29,341
It's funny how he keeps thinking like a painter.

302
00:12:30,811 --> 00:12:31,941
It's been ages since I last visited.

303
00:12:34,611 --> 00:12:35,651
(As Moomoo predicted, it's an art supply store.)

304
00:12:35,651 --> 00:12:37,181
This place is huge.

305
00:12:37,451 --> 00:12:39,121
I didn't know there was a store like that.

306
00:12:43,661 --> 00:12:44,821
It's been a while.

307
00:12:46,891 --> 00:12:48,091
They still sell the plaster busts.

308
00:12:48,091 --> 00:12:49,261
- Of course. - Yes.

309
00:12:50,501 --> 00:12:51,831
Arias.

310
00:12:52,631 --> 00:12:54,531
Arias is the only one that comes in medium.

311
00:12:55,471 --> 00:12:57,371
- They have names? - Of course.

312
00:12:57,571 --> 00:13:00,011
- She's probably a princess. - I see.

313
00:13:01,411 --> 00:13:04,741
One of the things I've worked hardest on in my life...

314
00:13:04,841 --> 00:13:06,081
was drawing plaster busts.

315
00:13:06,311 --> 00:13:08,581
- I did that for my college exam. - Look how young he looks.

316
00:13:08,581 --> 00:13:09,951
That's how I got into college.

317
00:13:10,121 --> 00:13:11,381
That was the exam.

318
00:13:14,291 --> 00:13:15,291
That was a long time ago.

319
00:13:15,291 --> 00:13:16,291
- To get into an art school, - That was the exam?

320
00:13:16,291 --> 00:13:17,761
everyone practices that.

321
00:13:17,921 --> 00:13:20,631
It's been over 20 years since I stopped working...

322
00:13:20,631 --> 00:13:22,301
to help students get into college.

323
00:13:22,731 --> 00:13:24,931
But I always drew one every 3 to 4 years.

324
00:13:25,261 --> 00:13:26,871
I loved sketching plaster busts.

325
00:13:27,401 --> 00:13:28,701
Pencil drawing is...

326
00:13:28,701 --> 00:13:31,901
the most basic form of art to observe and depict objects.

327
00:13:32,141 --> 00:13:35,641
Every athlete does weights...

328
00:13:35,741 --> 00:13:38,511
whatever sport they play. It's equivalent to that.

329
00:13:38,541 --> 00:13:40,151
To improve my basic skills, I wanted to draw one...

330
00:13:40,211 --> 00:13:41,711
- after a long while. - You're going back to the basics.

331
00:13:41,711 --> 00:13:43,621
- So I bought a bust of Arias. - Yes.

332
00:13:43,721 --> 00:13:45,951
And I love her smile. When you look at her lips,

333
00:13:46,851 --> 00:13:48,051
they curl up like this.

334
00:13:49,121 --> 00:13:50,921
I like her lips.

335
00:13:50,961 --> 00:13:52,231
The smirking princess.

336
00:13:54,131 --> 00:13:55,231
Not a lot of people buy this, right?

337
00:13:55,731 --> 00:13:56,861
- Actually, people buy... - Really?

338
00:13:56,861 --> 00:13:57,961
Arias more than you think.

339
00:13:57,961 --> 00:13:59,201
I guess people still do pencil sketching.

340
00:13:59,201 --> 00:14:01,731
People buy this more than you think.

341
00:14:02,241 --> 00:14:03,441
Thank you, sir.

342
00:14:03,501 --> 00:14:05,671
Do you have a small easel?

343
00:14:06,141 --> 00:14:08,511
- Which one? An easel? - Something like this.

344
00:14:08,711 --> 00:14:09,941
I should get one of that.

345
00:14:11,181 --> 00:14:12,551
- Thank you, sir. - Sure.

346
00:14:12,551 --> 00:14:14,351
- Have fun. Thank you. - Okay.

347
00:14:14,551 --> 00:14:16,151
You look like an art student.

348
00:14:16,321 --> 00:14:18,451
- Is that so? - He used to go to an art school.

349
00:14:19,291 --> 00:14:21,351
(Like an art student, he knows what to do.)

350
00:14:21,351 --> 00:14:22,361
I want a 6B pencil.

351
00:14:23,621 --> 00:14:25,861
Could you give me a sheet holder for the easel pads?

352
00:14:25,861 --> 00:14:27,161
- My gosh. - Oh, my.

353
00:14:27,331 --> 00:14:29,201
Yes. People look artsy with the holder.

354
00:14:29,201 --> 00:14:30,431
- He does look like an art student. - Do you have Kent paper?

355
00:14:30,431 --> 00:14:32,301
Kent paper? This way, please.

356
00:14:34,931 --> 00:14:36,971
(He takes a look at them.)

357
00:14:37,871 --> 00:14:38,911
All done.

358
00:14:39,911 --> 00:14:40,941
Thank you.

359
00:14:40,941 --> 00:14:41,971
(He places the paper into the holder.)

360
00:14:41,971 --> 00:14:43,341
It had been a while since I held that.

361
00:14:43,641 --> 00:14:44,711
Have a good day.

362
00:14:45,581 --> 00:14:48,681
(He swiftly buys everything he needs.)

363
00:14:49,751 --> 00:14:51,751
Aren't you registered in our system?

364
00:14:52,191 --> 00:14:53,351
I did spend a lot of money here.

365
00:14:53,351 --> 00:14:54,391
I'm a VIP customer there.

366
00:14:55,861 --> 00:14:57,061
Of course, you are.

367
00:14:57,291 --> 00:14:58,491
- I'm not? - No.

368
00:14:59,231 --> 00:15:00,961
You should sign up. They give you a discount.

369
00:15:02,561 --> 00:15:05,931
Do students draw plaster busts to get into art high schools?

370
00:15:06,071 --> 00:15:07,171
They don't do that for college, right?

371
00:15:07,171 --> 00:15:08,171
- Right. - Right?

372
00:15:09,341 --> 00:15:10,441
Have a good day.

373
00:15:10,441 --> 00:15:11,441
(The former art instructor knows everything.)

374
00:15:14,841 --> 00:15:18,381
(He's getting into a taxi again?)

375
00:15:19,451 --> 00:15:20,451
Hello.

376
00:15:20,651 --> 00:15:22,851
(Struggling)

377
00:15:22,981 --> 00:15:24,821
- That's big indeed. - That's huge.

378
00:15:24,821 --> 00:15:26,921
It's medium-sized, so it's pretty big.

379
00:15:26,921 --> 00:15:28,521
It's like two people are getting in.

380
00:15:28,861 --> 00:15:30,161
She's sitting next to him?

381
00:15:35,131 --> 00:15:37,661
(It's taking some time to reach the next destination.)

382
00:15:37,661 --> 00:15:40,171
- Hanam? - How far are you going?

383
00:15:40,471 --> 00:15:41,671
You can stop over there, sir.

384
00:15:41,671 --> 00:15:42,901
- It's here, right? - Yes.

385
00:15:44,401 --> 00:15:45,441
- What's in Hanam? - You...

386
00:15:45,441 --> 00:15:46,841
Did he move to Hanam without telling us again?

387
00:15:46,841 --> 00:15:47,971
- He moved to Hanam? - No.

388
00:15:49,541 --> 00:15:50,941
(Looking around)

389
00:15:51,011 --> 00:15:53,281
Is this the entrance? I've never been here before.

390
00:15:54,051 --> 00:15:55,051
Where is this place?

391
00:15:56,951 --> 00:15:57,981
Is this it?

392
00:15:59,491 --> 00:16:01,791
(Ringing)

393
00:16:04,191 --> 00:16:06,091
- Kian84. - Hey, it's been a while.

394
00:16:07,191 --> 00:16:08,191
- What? - It's been a while.

395
00:16:08,191 --> 00:16:09,761
- My goodness. - Hey, it's been a while.

396
00:16:09,761 --> 00:16:12,531
- It's Chung Jae. My gosh. - It's been a while.

397
00:16:15,331 --> 00:16:17,801
Mr. Starlight. I haven't heard this song in a while.

398
00:16:18,071 --> 00:16:20,671
- It's Chung Jae. My gosh. - Yes.

399
00:16:21,311 --> 00:16:23,311
I was stressed out from painting.

400
00:16:23,841 --> 00:16:27,281
So I naturally missed Chung Jae.

401
00:16:27,751 --> 00:16:29,381
There's the bright glow in his eyes.

402
00:16:29,381 --> 00:16:31,051
When you're stressed, you must look at beautiful things.

403
00:16:31,051 --> 00:16:32,321
Only his clips get a slow-motion effect.

404
00:16:32,621 --> 00:16:33,991
He looks cool even when playing catch.

405
00:16:35,291 --> 00:16:36,691
I don't end up with a solution.

406
00:16:37,091 --> 00:16:38,421
But he listens to my problems.

407
00:16:38,561 --> 00:16:40,231
He sympathizes with me.

408
00:16:41,191 --> 00:16:42,801
When was that?

409
00:16:42,801 --> 00:16:44,401
- That was a long time ago. - My gosh.

410
00:16:44,401 --> 00:16:45,601
But he hasn't aged at all.

411
00:16:45,601 --> 00:16:47,671
- My goodness. That's me. - He looks exactly the same.

412
00:16:47,671 --> 00:16:48,701
That's Na Rae.

413
00:16:48,771 --> 00:16:51,301
(Their first meeting was full of excitement.)

414
00:16:51,301 --> 00:16:52,341
(Look at Na Rae!)

415
00:16:52,341 --> 00:16:53,541
- Her face is red. - Hello.

416
00:16:54,411 --> 00:16:55,511
(Yes, I remember that day.)

417
00:16:55,511 --> 00:16:56,641
(Walking down memory lane)

418
00:16:56,641 --> 00:16:58,041
He looks exactly the same.

419
00:16:58,041 --> 00:16:59,551
He hasn't aged at all. His skin is still milky white.

420
00:16:59,951 --> 00:17:01,481
He's indeed handsome.

421
00:17:01,811 --> 00:17:03,921
What's the age difference between you...

422
00:17:03,921 --> 00:17:05,151
and Chung Jae?

423
00:17:05,151 --> 00:17:06,391
Do you want to guess?

424
00:17:06,421 --> 00:17:07,891
- Well... - Gosh.

425
00:17:07,891 --> 00:17:09,591
- What a lame question. - Be honest.

426
00:17:10,091 --> 00:17:11,121
- About seven years? - What?

427
00:17:11,461 --> 00:17:12,761
This is so annoying.

428
00:17:14,031 --> 00:17:16,001
Stop running now!

429
00:17:16,001 --> 00:17:17,501
I do agree that I look much older.

430
00:17:17,501 --> 00:17:18,861
- How many years are you apart? - Only two years.

431
00:17:18,861 --> 00:17:21,071
- No! Hold on. - My gosh.

432
00:17:21,071 --> 00:17:23,871
- I'm so sorry! Oh, no. - How could you tease him like that?

433
00:17:23,871 --> 00:17:25,871
- No. I meant... - My gosh. No.

434
00:17:26,041 --> 00:17:27,171
Why are we shaking hands?

435
00:17:27,171 --> 00:17:28,411
Are you trying to make it go away?

436
00:17:28,411 --> 00:17:29,681
- Lee Jung. - Get your hand away!

437
00:17:29,741 --> 00:17:31,141
Lee Jung. He just looks younger than his age.

438
00:17:31,141 --> 00:17:32,911
- Right. How can he look that young? - Right?

439
00:17:32,911 --> 00:17:34,551
I was actually going to say five years.

440
00:17:34,551 --> 00:17:36,021
- I wanted to be funny. - Yes.

441
00:17:36,081 --> 00:17:37,221
You're so mean.

442
00:17:37,481 --> 00:17:39,821
- I was trying to be funny. - Yes.

443
00:17:39,951 --> 00:17:42,151
- I believe you. - I mean it. I'm serious.

444
00:17:42,321 --> 00:17:45,731
He's the perfect art student people dream of.

445
00:17:45,731 --> 00:17:47,191
He's the perfect example of that.

446
00:17:47,191 --> 00:17:48,391
- Right. He is. - That's right.

447
00:17:48,531 --> 00:17:49,831
You know what I read online?

448
00:17:50,331 --> 00:17:53,201
Someone asked, "Will I see people like Chung Jae at an art college?"

449
00:17:53,301 --> 00:17:55,641
"There are guys from Kian1 to Kian84."

450
00:17:55,641 --> 00:17:57,471
(There are guys from Kian1 to Kian84.)

451
00:17:58,071 --> 00:18:00,271
- That comment hurt my feelings. - From Kian1?

452
00:18:00,271 --> 00:18:02,441
Kian1, Kian2, Kian3, Kian4...

453
00:18:02,441 --> 00:18:03,881
- Kian75... - Like "Mickey 17?"

454
00:18:03,881 --> 00:18:05,311
- Yes. - "Mickey 17."

455
00:18:05,811 --> 00:18:07,781
I was feeling good today.

456
00:18:07,781 --> 00:18:08,951
This ruined my mood now.

457
00:18:09,181 --> 00:18:11,251
So which numbered Kian is the best?

458
00:18:11,251 --> 00:18:13,251
Of course, it's Kian84. Obviously.

459
00:18:13,251 --> 00:18:15,321
- But... - I think Kian84 is the best one.

460
00:18:15,921 --> 00:18:17,121
He's the best.

461
00:18:17,521 --> 00:18:18,721
Gosh, this is the place?

462
00:18:18,721 --> 00:18:20,591
- It's nice with the high ceiling. - Gosh, no.

463
00:18:21,631 --> 00:18:23,361
(Happy to see him)

464
00:18:24,401 --> 00:18:25,701
How long has it been?

465
00:18:25,701 --> 00:18:27,401
My goodness.

466
00:18:27,431 --> 00:18:30,271
We met at my exhibit last year. Right?

467
00:18:30,271 --> 00:18:31,271
Yes.

468
00:18:31,271 --> 00:18:33,111
It's been one and a half years since then.

469
00:18:33,571 --> 00:18:34,641
It's been that long?

470
00:18:34,841 --> 00:18:35,841
Yes.

471
00:18:36,081 --> 00:18:37,411
Why do you look so tanned?

472
00:18:37,481 --> 00:18:39,251
I've been running a lot these days.

473
00:18:40,451 --> 00:18:42,351
- You got slim and tanned. - Yes.

474
00:18:42,351 --> 00:18:44,781
- Did he get facial treatments? - He doesn't get stuff like that.

475
00:18:45,021 --> 00:18:46,591
He doesn't even apply lotion.

476
00:18:46,591 --> 00:18:48,721
It looks like he barely goes out in the sun.

477
00:18:48,791 --> 00:18:51,321
You know what? If he runs three marathons,

478
00:18:51,321 --> 00:18:53,061
he'll get tanned and lose his sparkle.

479
00:18:55,531 --> 00:18:56,631
What have you been up to?

480
00:18:56,861 --> 00:18:58,861
- I recently did a group exhibit. - Okay.

481
00:18:58,861 --> 00:19:00,571
The pile over there is from the group exhibit.

482
00:19:00,831 --> 00:19:02,341
I'm sorry I couldn't attend your exhibit.

483
00:19:02,341 --> 00:19:03,741
It was a group exhibit.

484
00:19:03,801 --> 00:19:05,811
- It was iffy to invite you. - Okay.

485
00:19:05,811 --> 00:19:07,611
But it still turned out well.

486
00:19:07,811 --> 00:19:09,381
Anything else?

487
00:19:09,681 --> 00:19:11,711
I've been working since then.

488
00:19:11,711 --> 00:19:13,511
- I've been painting. - Have you been painting?

489
00:19:13,951 --> 00:19:16,481
Hey, give me a tour of your studio.

490
00:19:16,481 --> 00:19:17,921
I need you to explain what's what.

491
00:19:23,021 --> 00:19:24,961
- His studio has gotten bigger. - Yes.

492
00:19:25,691 --> 00:19:27,591
(He's been holding exhibits regularly as an active artist.)

493
00:19:27,591 --> 00:19:29,661
- He's focusing only on his art. - Right.

494
00:19:29,731 --> 00:19:31,331
He's known as the handsome art major.

495
00:19:31,331 --> 00:19:33,371
I'm at Chung Jae's studio now.

496
00:19:33,801 --> 00:19:36,671
He moved his studio about three years ago.

497
00:19:36,671 --> 00:19:38,871
I've been busy with work,

498
00:19:38,871 --> 00:19:40,671
so it took me a long time to visit his studio.

499
00:19:40,811 --> 00:19:42,341
(This is nice.)

500
00:19:42,341 --> 00:19:43,641
And on this side,

501
00:19:44,411 --> 00:19:45,951
I have the kiln in the back.

502
00:19:46,051 --> 00:19:48,011
(His studio is also equipped with a kiln.)

503
00:19:48,011 --> 00:19:49,681
- He does all kinds of art. - For pottery?

504
00:19:49,821 --> 00:19:51,351
- That's after the bisque firing. - Okay.

505
00:19:51,551 --> 00:19:52,791
- That's how it looks. - You changed the shape.

506
00:19:53,351 --> 00:19:55,761
I've been trying out different shapes.

507
00:19:56,621 --> 00:19:57,921
- You're over there. At the top. - My gosh.

508
00:19:58,291 --> 00:20:00,261
That looks so eerie. Why does it look so eerie?

509
00:20:00,261 --> 00:20:01,361
- It looks so eerie. - My gosh.

510
00:20:02,061 --> 00:20:03,631
(Kian84, sleeping in the art studio)

511
00:20:03,631 --> 00:20:04,831
- Hold on. - What's that?

512
00:20:04,831 --> 00:20:06,431
A long time ago when I visited his studio,

513
00:20:06,431 --> 00:20:08,131
we created my bust together.

514
00:20:08,131 --> 00:20:09,641
- What was that called? - That's the bust.

515
00:20:09,641 --> 00:20:11,101
The things at the Mausoleum of Qin Shi Huang in China...

516
00:20:11,171 --> 00:20:12,341
- The Terracotta Army? - Yes.

517
00:20:12,341 --> 00:20:13,841
- But... - In the Terracotta Army,

518
00:20:13,841 --> 00:20:15,141
there's a soldier with that face.

519
00:20:16,441 --> 00:20:18,241
Was that a meaningful project?

520
00:20:18,241 --> 00:20:19,451
- That's... - Creating a bust together?

521
00:20:19,551 --> 00:20:20,581
That was a long time ago.

522
00:20:20,581 --> 00:20:23,821
One time, he was working on sculptures.

523
00:20:23,821 --> 00:20:25,221
- He did a great job. - I asked him to make one for me.

524
00:20:25,221 --> 00:20:26,991
- That's so fascinating. - It looks just like him.

525
00:20:26,991 --> 00:20:28,121
It looks just like him.

526
00:20:28,291 --> 00:20:29,321
(Rummaging through)

527
00:20:29,321 --> 00:20:31,521
- So how is it going for you? - What's that?

528
00:20:31,791 --> 00:20:33,631
How's your art coming along?

529
00:20:33,861 --> 00:20:36,031
I wouldn't say it's going well.

530
00:20:36,031 --> 00:20:37,401
- Okay. - But I'm grateful...

531
00:20:37,401 --> 00:20:38,431
that I can continue.

532
00:20:38,961 --> 00:20:40,001
Really?

533
00:20:40,301 --> 00:20:41,431
He's so cool.

534
00:20:41,671 --> 00:20:42,971
He says the coolest things too.

535
00:20:42,971 --> 00:20:44,401
- He's cool. - But that's a fact.

536
00:20:44,401 --> 00:20:46,711
Good-looking people are not as ill-tempered.

537
00:20:46,711 --> 00:20:48,671
- Of course. That's because... - They grew up with love.

538
00:20:48,671 --> 00:20:50,441
- everyone is nice to them. - Yes.

539
00:20:50,841 --> 00:20:52,611
I doubt they had to face any injustice.

540
00:20:52,611 --> 00:20:55,551
That's why when I'm stressed out, I go to Chung Jae.

541
00:20:55,751 --> 00:20:57,121
When I see him, it softens my anger.

542
00:20:57,121 --> 00:20:58,251
Yes. That's right.

543
00:20:58,251 --> 00:20:59,491
- You used to do that? - Yes.

544
00:20:59,991 --> 00:21:01,451
Your visit must've stressed him out.

545
00:21:01,551 --> 00:21:04,091
Chung Jae? Gosh, I'm not that ugly.

546
00:21:04,691 --> 00:21:07,461
- I look pretty good too. - Come on, don't be so mean.

547
00:21:08,261 --> 00:21:12,331
(Kian84 shyly glances at Chung Jae.)

548
00:21:13,701 --> 00:21:15,401
Chung Jae, here. I've got a present for you.

549
00:21:17,141 --> 00:21:18,901
It's my first time visiting your studio.

550
00:21:20,111 --> 00:21:21,311
You can open it.

551
00:21:22,041 --> 00:21:23,641
I rarely do stuff like this.

552
00:21:23,641 --> 00:21:25,581
- What's that? - What is it?

553
00:21:25,581 --> 00:21:27,281
This brings good luck.

554
00:21:28,381 --> 00:21:29,821
It's a pollack.

555
00:21:30,421 --> 00:21:31,751
- My gosh. - Yes, that's cute.

556
00:21:31,751 --> 00:21:33,521
That was so thoughtful.

557
00:21:33,591 --> 00:21:35,551
- The real fish might've smelled. - Yes.

558
00:21:35,551 --> 00:21:36,561
- That's cute. - That's better.

559
00:21:36,561 --> 00:21:38,361
- Right. This is nice. - Yes.

560
00:21:38,591 --> 00:21:40,861
- Luck. - Bring me a lot of luck.

561
00:21:41,891 --> 00:21:42,901
It jingles too.

562
00:21:42,901 --> 00:21:44,731
A lot of people give that out as a present these days.

563
00:21:44,731 --> 00:21:45,831
- It's a big trend. - Yes.

564
00:21:48,831 --> 00:21:50,471
I brought the plaster bust today,

565
00:21:50,701 --> 00:21:52,101
so we could draw together.

566
00:21:52,101 --> 00:21:54,471
- Draw the plaster bust? - Yes.

567
00:21:54,711 --> 00:21:57,081
- That's why you brought Arias? - I brought Arias.

568
00:21:57,141 --> 00:21:59,381
It's almost been two centuries since I last saw it.

569
00:21:59,451 --> 00:22:02,011
(He comes to face her after a long time.)

570
00:22:03,581 --> 00:22:05,421
- She's expensive these days. - How much?

571
00:22:05,651 --> 00:22:07,391
This used to be less than 30 dollars, right?

572
00:22:07,391 --> 00:22:08,551
It's 70 dollars now.

573
00:22:10,461 --> 00:22:13,191
But the price didn't go up that much considering the inflation rate.

574
00:22:13,891 --> 00:22:15,261
You carried this from the supply store?

575
00:22:15,261 --> 00:22:16,361
I did.

576
00:22:16,861 --> 00:22:18,801
Hey, let's draw her together.

577
00:22:19,501 --> 00:22:20,771
- I'm drawing her too? - Yes,

578
00:22:20,771 --> 00:22:22,031
you should since I'll be drawing her.

579
00:22:22,531 --> 00:22:25,401
You need to brush up on your basic skills too.

580
00:22:25,401 --> 00:22:27,511
It's been so long since I last did one.

581
00:22:27,641 --> 00:22:30,981
- I'm not sure if I can do it well. - It's been 6 to 7 years for me too.

582
00:22:31,581 --> 00:22:34,211
But It's fun to do it at times.

583
00:22:34,851 --> 00:22:36,151
Okay then.

584
00:22:36,281 --> 00:22:37,281
He accepted it.

585
00:22:37,281 --> 00:22:38,921
I wanted to draw with him.

586
00:22:39,081 --> 00:22:41,221
He did cast drawing in the past, after all.

587
00:22:41,291 --> 00:22:43,121
When I ask Chung Jae to do something with me,

588
00:22:43,991 --> 00:22:45,691
he obliges without a complaint.

589
00:22:46,731 --> 00:22:47,731
He's like that.

590
00:22:47,731 --> 00:22:48,731
(That's why I'm always thankful.)

591
00:22:48,861 --> 00:22:51,001
Thank you for doing this with me today, Chung Jae.

592
00:22:52,061 --> 00:22:53,601
Doing this so suddenly...

593
00:22:54,571 --> 00:22:56,071
makes me feel like an instructor at an academy.

594
00:22:57,641 --> 00:22:59,711
We worked together at an academy back in the day.

595
00:23:00,341 --> 00:23:02,241
I bet it brings back memories.

596
00:23:02,741 --> 00:23:05,981
We did similar things together when we were younger,

597
00:23:06,481 --> 00:23:08,281
so the activity put me at ease.

598
00:23:08,281 --> 00:23:10,051
There are 4B and 6B pencils.

599
00:23:10,051 --> 00:23:13,621
(They sharpen the pencils as they think about the past.)

600
00:23:14,991 --> 00:23:16,821
(Plopping down)

601
00:23:17,161 --> 00:23:19,291
(The two artists sit across from each other.)

602
00:23:20,361 --> 00:23:21,891
It's like a youth drama.

603
00:23:21,891 --> 00:23:23,961
Chung Jae looks like a character in a movie.

604
00:23:25,131 --> 00:23:27,401
- It's slow motion. - What?

605
00:23:27,401 --> 00:23:28,631
You don't look bad.

606
00:23:29,231 --> 00:23:32,401
(Let's do a good job.)

607
00:23:33,771 --> 00:23:35,041
(Laughing)

608
00:23:36,281 --> 00:23:37,541
Why is this so funny?

609
00:23:38,441 --> 00:23:40,281
(Doing the basic drawing again makes him embarrassed.)

610
00:23:41,111 --> 00:23:42,181
Hey, it's nice.

611
00:23:42,181 --> 00:23:44,951
It has been so long since I did this.

612
00:23:45,621 --> 00:23:47,721
- Don't you need an extender? - What's an extender?

613
00:23:48,021 --> 00:23:50,861
You put it at the end of the pencil so that it becomes longer.

614
00:23:51,221 --> 00:23:52,361
You use it for short pencils.

615
00:23:53,061 --> 00:23:56,761
(Rummaging)

616
00:23:57,201 --> 00:23:59,061
(He heats up a pen.)

617
00:23:59,061 --> 00:24:00,731
- All right. - What's up with the lighter?

618
00:24:01,501 --> 00:24:03,441
- What's that for? - People did that back in the day.

619
00:24:03,441 --> 00:24:07,571
People used pens and markers as an extender.

620
00:24:07,571 --> 00:24:08,841
- The old markers. - Yes.

621
00:24:08,841 --> 00:24:11,011
Since I didn't have an extender, I made one with what I had.

622
00:24:11,011 --> 00:24:13,251
Why do you put an extender on the pencil?

623
00:24:13,251 --> 00:24:15,811
It increases the radius of rotation.

624
00:24:15,811 --> 00:24:18,351
- The length. - You can cover more area at once.

625
00:24:19,121 --> 00:24:20,821
The radius increases.

626
00:24:20,921 --> 00:24:22,761
It has been a while, so the extender looks strange.

627
00:24:23,621 --> 00:24:25,261
Why did it come out so lousy?

628
00:24:27,831 --> 00:24:29,531
This pencil is short.

629
00:24:31,331 --> 00:24:32,561
Come on.

630
00:24:32,731 --> 00:24:34,801
Why is the BGM going, "Starlight is falling?"

631
00:24:34,801 --> 00:24:36,601
- It did fall on his face. - His face?

632
00:24:38,441 --> 00:24:40,411
(It has been a while since he held a pencil for drawing.)

633
00:24:40,511 --> 00:24:42,141
Kian84 is sitting in a dark spot.

634
00:24:42,141 --> 00:24:43,641
That's right. He's sitting in a dark spot.

635
00:24:43,641 --> 00:24:45,341
Everyone, please focus on the drawing.

636
00:24:45,341 --> 00:24:47,081
That's what you should focus on.

637
00:24:47,281 --> 00:24:50,521
I don't even remember how I did it in the past.

638
00:24:51,021 --> 00:24:52,081
Really?

639
00:24:52,221 --> 00:24:53,751
How did I draw it?

640
00:24:53,751 --> 00:24:55,851
(How did I draw it?)

641
00:24:57,121 --> 00:24:58,991
(Glancing over)

642
00:24:58,991 --> 00:25:01,961
It was fun to do it again after a while.

643
00:25:02,861 --> 00:25:04,361
I bet it brought back memories.

644
00:25:07,031 --> 00:25:08,501
It's the healthy kind of therapeutic time.

645
00:25:10,901 --> 00:25:15,211
It seems like you always miss the time you worked...

646
00:25:15,841 --> 00:25:18,041
- at the art academy. - Me?

647
00:25:18,311 --> 00:25:20,481
Why am I always hung up on the past?

648
00:25:20,481 --> 00:25:21,651
Everyone is like that.

649
00:25:22,311 --> 00:25:26,451
Your desire to do the cast drawing tells me...

650
00:25:26,451 --> 00:25:29,191
that you can't forget the past.

651
00:25:29,191 --> 00:25:30,261
Back then...

652
00:25:30,261 --> 00:25:31,961
(You have a point.)

653
00:25:31,961 --> 00:25:34,161
You have a point. It's...

654
00:25:35,231 --> 00:25:36,961
one of the things I worked hard on in my life.

655
00:25:37,461 --> 00:25:40,571
It hurts my shoulders and arms more than I remember.

656
00:25:43,641 --> 00:25:45,441
(Despite the hardships,)

657
00:25:45,441 --> 00:25:47,211
(the two artists draw as they did in the past.)

658
00:25:48,171 --> 00:25:49,211
(Decalcomania)

659
00:25:49,211 --> 00:25:50,241
We look similar.

660
00:25:51,411 --> 00:25:53,581
I'll need to visit the studio twice more to complete the drawing.

661
00:25:54,551 --> 00:25:57,821
(He tries to go back to the time when he was a passionate rookie.)

662
00:25:58,721 --> 00:25:59,921
It's hard.

663
00:26:01,751 --> 00:26:03,721
Chung Jae, let's order some food.

664
00:26:03,961 --> 00:26:05,621
Okay. What should we order?

665
00:26:06,261 --> 00:26:08,261
Chinese food. Can you order it?

666
00:26:09,831 --> 00:26:11,661
Of course. What would you like?

667
00:26:12,561 --> 00:26:14,831
- Stir-fried jjajangmyeon. - Stir-fried jjajangmyeon?

668
00:26:15,501 --> 00:26:17,441
I'll order tangsuyuk as well.

669
00:26:17,441 --> 00:26:18,441
Okay.

670
00:26:18,841 --> 00:26:20,071
- I ordered it. - Okay.

671
00:26:20,071 --> 00:26:22,641
- What? Did Chung Jae pay? - Yes, Chung Jae paid.

672
00:26:23,881 --> 00:26:25,511
Before we eat...

673
00:26:27,281 --> 00:26:29,621
(Let's focus.)

674
00:26:30,721 --> 00:26:32,281
(Rustling)

675
00:26:33,621 --> 00:26:35,721
(Arias begins to take shape.)

676
00:26:37,421 --> 00:26:38,961
(Focused)

677
00:26:39,791 --> 00:26:41,891
- It's nice to see you so focused. - Yes.

678
00:26:42,391 --> 00:26:44,201
- You look relaxed. - That's right.

679
00:26:44,201 --> 00:26:46,501
- His facial expression changed... - Yes.

680
00:26:46,771 --> 00:26:47,971
from the time when he was at home.

681
00:26:48,631 --> 00:26:49,771
He looks so happy.

682
00:26:49,771 --> 00:26:50,771
(Winking)

683
00:26:51,141 --> 00:26:52,801
You're right.

684
00:26:53,611 --> 00:26:55,011
You aren't swearing.

685
00:26:55,371 --> 00:26:57,811
You look more cheerful and joyous than you did...

686
00:26:57,811 --> 00:27:00,051
- when you drew earlier. - He isn't stressed out now.

687
00:27:00,051 --> 00:27:01,451
- It was fun. - Right.

688
00:27:07,251 --> 00:27:09,621
Kian84, you should've closed one eye.

689
00:27:09,621 --> 00:27:12,121
- That's right. - Why is my tongue coming out?

690
00:27:13,631 --> 00:27:16,361
(He goes back to his rookie days and focuses on the drawing.)

691
00:27:16,361 --> 00:27:18,161
- You're good. - It's fun.

692
00:27:18,161 --> 00:27:19,331
That's his talent.

693
00:27:19,731 --> 00:27:21,431
- How fascinating. - It looks incredible.

694
00:27:21,531 --> 00:27:22,871
It's really fascinating.

695
00:27:22,871 --> 00:27:25,371
(Time passes quickly like the bygone days.)

696
00:27:26,211 --> 00:27:28,741
How did I do this in four hours?

697
00:27:30,111 --> 00:27:31,841
- It's really hard. - Right?

698
00:27:32,981 --> 00:27:34,081
The college entrance exam...

699
00:27:34,081 --> 00:27:35,281
(The two artists are exhausted as they haven't drawn in a while.)

700
00:27:35,281 --> 00:27:36,521
was strange.

701
00:27:37,351 --> 00:27:39,851
At first, Chung Jae played around.

702
00:27:39,851 --> 00:27:41,391
- But he became absorbed soon. - You're right.

703
00:27:41,391 --> 00:27:44,421
When you made your artworks, you felt the pain of creation.

704
00:27:44,421 --> 00:27:45,861
- You put gesso over your work. - Yes.

705
00:27:45,991 --> 00:27:47,561
Was your heart at ease back then?

706
00:27:48,191 --> 00:27:49,731
- It was fun. - You found it fun.

707
00:27:49,731 --> 00:27:52,601
I enjoyed it. It was like playing a game.

708
00:27:53,601 --> 00:27:56,701
(As if they're playing a game, they enjoy drawing.)

709
00:27:56,701 --> 00:27:59,371
That means you aren't fed up with arts.

710
00:27:59,371 --> 00:28:00,371
That's right.

711
00:28:00,371 --> 00:28:02,771
- It's fun because it isn't work. - I guess so.

712
00:28:02,771 --> 00:28:04,311
In the industry Lee Jung and I are in,

713
00:28:04,311 --> 00:28:07,051
working on the choreography of the title song is...

714
00:28:07,051 --> 00:28:08,851
so pressuring that it drives me crazy.

715
00:28:08,851 --> 00:28:09,921
- It's work. - That's right.

716
00:28:09,921 --> 00:28:11,821
- That's right. - One song must show everything.

717
00:28:11,821 --> 00:28:14,791
But when we work on other tracks of the album...

718
00:28:14,791 --> 00:28:16,291
- We're much more at ease. - There's no pressure whatsoever...

719
00:28:16,291 --> 00:28:18,261
- That's right. - when we practice.

720
00:28:18,261 --> 00:28:19,261
- It's the same. - So,

721
00:28:19,261 --> 00:28:21,391
- the artists and I have fun. - That's right.

722
00:28:21,391 --> 00:28:23,731
We get excited and work freely.

723
00:28:24,301 --> 00:28:25,361
- That's right. - Yes.

724
00:28:26,131 --> 00:28:27,231
(They draw in a relaxed way without any pressure.)

725
00:28:27,231 --> 00:28:28,501
I'm hungry. I hope the food arrives soon.

726
00:28:29,741 --> 00:28:30,941
(Staring)

727
00:28:30,941 --> 00:28:32,271
- What? It's here. - Really?

728
00:28:32,641 --> 00:28:33,911
When did the food get here?

729
00:28:34,041 --> 00:28:35,741
(They were so focused that they didn't know it was here.)

730
00:28:35,741 --> 00:28:37,011
I didn't know that it was here.

731
00:28:39,381 --> 00:28:42,051
(They have a meal after awakening the drawing senses.)

732
00:28:43,451 --> 00:28:44,651
Did you order regular jjajangmyeon?

733
00:28:44,981 --> 00:28:46,591
I ordered the regular one.

734
00:28:47,051 --> 00:28:48,151
I'm exhausted.

735
00:28:57,361 --> 00:28:59,601
These days, I can't even draw for five hours a day.

736
00:29:00,901 --> 00:29:04,671
I keep doing different things,

737
00:29:06,941 --> 00:29:09,071
so my ability to concentrate on my artwork...

738
00:29:09,071 --> 00:29:11,311
keeps suffering.

739
00:29:11,711 --> 00:29:14,551
Back in the day when I drew webtoons,

740
00:29:14,881 --> 00:29:16,421
I could draw for 12 hours a day.

741
00:29:18,121 --> 00:29:20,291
You were young then.

742
00:29:22,851 --> 00:29:26,091
Until 3 or 4 years ago,

743
00:29:27,091 --> 00:29:28,231
I could draw...

744
00:29:29,061 --> 00:29:30,561
like a machine.

745
00:29:30,931 --> 00:29:32,101
You're incredible.

746
00:29:32,101 --> 00:29:33,771
When it comes to fine arts,

747
00:29:34,871 --> 00:29:38,341
I've only done it for 3 to 4 years.

748
00:29:39,241 --> 00:29:41,371
At times, I don't even know what I'm doing.

749
00:29:43,511 --> 00:29:45,011
(I see.)

750
00:29:45,011 --> 00:29:47,211
I don't know if I should continue on with it.

751
00:29:48,251 --> 00:29:50,281
I just try to work hard since I've begun.

752
00:29:52,281 --> 00:29:55,221
(After unloading his worries,)

753
00:29:55,221 --> 00:29:57,121
(he finally takes a bite.)

754
00:29:57,661 --> 00:29:59,121
(Impressed)

755
00:29:59,121 --> 00:30:00,231
It's tasty.

756
00:30:04,731 --> 00:30:05,931
(Impressed)

757
00:30:08,971 --> 00:30:10,741
(Na Rae's mouth waters.)

758
00:30:10,741 --> 00:30:11,741
It looks tasty.

759
00:30:17,111 --> 00:30:21,051
(He eats hurriedly with jjajang on his mouth.)

760
00:30:25,881 --> 00:30:27,051
This year,

761
00:30:27,751 --> 00:30:29,491
I got so many offers to be on shows.

762
00:30:30,921 --> 00:30:32,691
Had I accepted all the offers,

763
00:30:32,691 --> 00:30:34,131
I would've been as busy as Hyun Moo.

764
00:30:37,331 --> 00:30:39,831
Had I accepted all the offers, I would've had no time to draw.

765
00:30:39,831 --> 00:30:41,231
So, I draw in my spare time.

766
00:30:42,401 --> 00:30:43,641
Making artworks...

767
00:30:43,641 --> 00:30:45,271
(Making artworks requires a lot of time.)

768
00:30:45,271 --> 00:30:46,441
requires a lot of time.

769
00:30:50,341 --> 00:30:51,741
(Sighing)

770
00:30:52,181 --> 00:30:53,851
Time is limited,

771
00:30:54,251 --> 00:30:55,711
but there are so many things I want to do.

772
00:30:55,711 --> 00:30:56,821
That's right.

773
00:30:57,421 --> 00:30:59,421
But if I'm not careful, I won't succeed in anything.

774
00:31:02,491 --> 00:31:05,421
You went there to unload the worries in your heart.

775
00:31:05,421 --> 00:31:06,861
That's right.

776
00:31:14,171 --> 00:31:16,741
Also, people say that running...

777
00:31:18,801 --> 00:31:20,471
makes your day lively by filling your body with energy...

778
00:31:20,471 --> 00:31:21,971
and helps you with work as a result.

779
00:31:22,571 --> 00:31:24,311
But I get so exhausted after running.

780
00:31:24,311 --> 00:31:27,211
I lose energy, so I can't work afterward.

781
00:31:27,211 --> 00:31:28,451
(I can't work afterward.)

782
00:31:28,451 --> 00:31:29,951
- Really? - Yes.

783
00:31:30,151 --> 00:31:31,881
Maybe it's a bad exercise for you.

784
00:31:32,421 --> 00:31:33,791
I lose energy.

785
00:31:34,791 --> 00:31:36,321
When you run...

786
00:31:36,321 --> 00:31:38,621
- So many people recommend it to me. - Right.

787
00:31:38,621 --> 00:31:41,591
All of them say, "Since you work so much,"

788
00:31:42,091 --> 00:31:44,401
"you should run and increase your stamina."

789
00:31:44,401 --> 00:31:45,401
- No. - No?

790
00:31:45,401 --> 00:31:46,901
- You'll die. - You'll lose your stamina.

791
00:31:46,901 --> 00:31:48,031
I'll lose my stamina?

792
00:31:48,401 --> 00:31:50,041
This lifestyle suits us.

793
00:31:50,041 --> 00:31:51,641
- Really? - Each person has the set amount...

794
00:31:51,641 --> 00:31:52,901
- of energy per day. - Energy.

795
00:31:52,901 --> 00:31:54,371
- That's right. - There's the set amount.

796
00:31:54,371 --> 00:31:56,071
One time, I ran in the morning with my dancer friend...

797
00:31:56,071 --> 00:31:57,381
and it ruined my entire day.

798
00:31:58,811 --> 00:32:00,251
- Really? - Yes.

799
00:32:00,251 --> 00:32:01,751
I'm at the important crossroads.

800
00:32:01,751 --> 00:32:04,421
I ran in the morning with Sean once, right?

801
00:32:04,421 --> 00:32:05,681
- Yes. - It ruined my entire day.

802
00:32:06,851 --> 00:32:08,351
It was too early.

803
00:32:08,351 --> 00:32:09,591
I could see that.

804
00:32:09,591 --> 00:32:11,661
It depends on the person.

805
00:32:11,661 --> 00:32:13,961
Some people gain energy...

806
00:32:13,961 --> 00:32:15,731
- That's right. - while others get tired.

807
00:32:16,801 --> 00:32:18,101
Really?

808
00:32:18,101 --> 00:32:19,601
- It makes no sense. - This is driving me crazy.

809
00:32:19,601 --> 00:32:21,631
What am I supposed to do? Should I run or not?

810
00:32:21,771 --> 00:32:23,971
I lose energy, so I can't work afterward.

811
00:32:23,971 --> 00:32:25,201
(The morning run makes him even more exhausted.)

812
00:32:26,401 --> 00:32:29,411
I thought running was your way of relieving stress.

813
00:32:30,011 --> 00:32:31,311
It does relieve stress.

814
00:32:31,311 --> 00:32:32,311
But...

815
00:32:33,381 --> 00:32:35,881
- it makes me lose time. - I see.

816
00:32:37,581 --> 00:32:39,321
That's the situation I'm in.

817
00:32:39,951 --> 00:32:41,091
It's understandable.

818
00:32:41,991 --> 00:32:43,861
(Nodding)

819
00:32:44,291 --> 00:32:45,391
If the Han River has...

820
00:32:46,261 --> 00:32:49,191
a wise man named Sean,

821
00:32:49,731 --> 00:32:53,771
Hanam has a wise man named Chung Jae.

822
00:32:54,171 --> 00:32:55,271
That's what I think.

823
00:32:55,431 --> 00:32:57,441
- He's a wise man. That's right. - A different kind of a wise man.

824
00:32:57,741 --> 00:33:00,911
When you think about it,

825
00:33:00,911 --> 00:33:02,371
when you look at him closely,

826
00:33:03,411 --> 00:33:05,841
he smiles like Buddha.

827
00:33:07,281 --> 00:33:08,511
(His smile is as kind as Buddha's.)

828
00:33:08,511 --> 00:33:10,151
His T-zone is fair.

829
00:33:11,121 --> 00:33:13,751
Poisonous thoughts well up inside me at times.

830
00:33:14,321 --> 00:33:16,561
But they go away when I meet him.

831
00:33:16,561 --> 00:33:18,361
- Chung Jae does detox. - That's right.

832
00:33:18,361 --> 00:33:20,231
- He has that effect on you. - That's right.

833
00:33:22,061 --> 00:33:23,091
(Impressed)

834
00:33:23,091 --> 00:33:24,261
You should meet him often.

835
00:33:25,431 --> 00:33:27,371
(He clears the bowl.)

836
00:33:28,671 --> 00:33:32,641
Last year, I counted my artworks after the exhibition.

837
00:33:32,841 --> 00:33:36,111
I had made fewer than 20 artworks in a year and a half.

838
00:33:36,541 --> 00:33:39,281
Aren't 20 artworks a lot?

839
00:33:39,781 --> 00:33:41,581
When I made webtoons,

840
00:33:42,051 --> 00:33:44,451
- I made over 100 cuts in a week - What?

841
00:33:44,621 --> 00:33:47,651
- I worked on a computer, - Webtoons are different.

842
00:33:48,091 --> 00:33:50,991
but I thought, "Even if I work on canvases,"

843
00:33:51,461 --> 00:33:54,331
"I'll easily make four artworks in a month."

844
00:33:55,291 --> 00:33:57,631
But... I actually worked fast in the beginning.

845
00:33:57,631 --> 00:33:59,031
I worked fast and produced many artworks.

846
00:33:59,161 --> 00:34:00,671
But as time goes on, my productivity decreases.

847
00:34:01,131 --> 00:34:04,041
Maybe you shouldn't compare your current self...

848
00:34:04,041 --> 00:34:05,171
to the past self.

849
00:34:05,171 --> 00:34:06,671
- That's right. - That's right.

850
00:34:06,841 --> 00:34:08,141
You made a good point.

851
00:34:08,311 --> 00:34:09,981
I sang "View" ten years ago.

852
00:34:10,681 --> 00:34:13,811
It's a beautiful memory. It was a moment of my youth.

853
00:34:13,811 --> 00:34:15,781
But I can't compare my current self to that version of me...

854
00:34:16,381 --> 00:34:18,351
because it makes me fester inside.

855
00:34:18,521 --> 00:34:20,091
- That's right. - I had to stop comparing the two.

856
00:34:20,091 --> 00:34:21,391
- It makes you fester inside. - Yes.

857
00:34:21,621 --> 00:34:24,761
You need to come up with the new self...

858
00:34:24,761 --> 00:34:27,531
that suits your current age and situation.

859
00:34:27,531 --> 00:34:29,091
- You made a good point. - That's right.

860
00:34:29,391 --> 00:34:30,661
KEY is absolutely right.

861
00:34:30,661 --> 00:34:33,201
Back when you made webtoons diligently,

862
00:34:33,371 --> 00:34:35,231
- you were only on this show. - That's right.

863
00:34:35,231 --> 00:34:37,571
- You looked happy when you drew. - That's right.

864
00:34:37,571 --> 00:34:38,771
The situation has changed.

865
00:34:38,771 --> 00:34:40,941
When you reach a certain age,

866
00:34:41,411 --> 00:34:43,071
you have so many responsibilities...

867
00:34:43,071 --> 00:34:44,081
even when you don't look for more work.

868
00:34:44,081 --> 00:34:45,111
So many.

869
00:34:45,211 --> 00:34:46,751
From the bills...

870
00:34:46,751 --> 00:34:49,181
to the family members, friends,

871
00:34:49,181 --> 00:34:51,351
and juniors as well as their issues...

872
00:34:51,881 --> 00:34:54,291
You get more responsibilities, and it's a natural course of events.

873
00:34:54,891 --> 00:34:56,521
- That's what I think. - That's right.

874
00:34:57,691 --> 00:35:00,031
You should set a different standard.

875
00:35:00,991 --> 00:35:03,161
(He had many pent-up worries.)

876
00:35:04,431 --> 00:35:06,001
I should let go of greed.

877
00:35:06,101 --> 00:35:07,531
- That's a good idea. - It's because of greed.

878
00:35:07,931 --> 00:35:09,231
(Nodding)

879
00:35:10,341 --> 00:35:14,411
(Meeting up with Chung Jae makes him open up his heart.)

880
00:35:17,011 --> 00:35:18,141
It was a nice meal.

881
00:35:20,381 --> 00:35:22,151
- This restaurant makes tasty food. - They're good.

882
00:35:23,681 --> 00:35:25,381
- Thank you for the food. - What?

883
00:35:26,551 --> 00:35:28,321
Why would you say that when you paid?

884
00:35:28,721 --> 00:35:30,021
I'm still thankful for the food.

885
00:35:31,221 --> 00:35:33,291
It must be nice to have a junior who buys you food.

886
00:35:34,131 --> 00:35:37,361
(Thank you. I'll take out the trash.)

887
00:35:37,731 --> 00:35:38,901
It's 6pm already.

888
00:35:38,901 --> 00:35:40,771
(Now that we've had a meal, shall we resume drawing?)

889
00:35:42,231 --> 00:35:44,641
(The two of them get back to work without resting.)

890
00:35:46,041 --> 00:35:49,641
(They become absorbed in drawing without any thoughts.)

891
00:35:50,711 --> 00:35:52,741
(They become true to the basics of the art.)

892
00:36:05,121 --> 00:36:08,491
(They draw without rest in the same spots.)

893
00:36:13,831 --> 00:36:16,941
We drew for three hours and a half.

894
00:36:18,371 --> 00:36:20,811
(They remain seated for over three hours.)

895
00:36:21,341 --> 00:36:22,571
Hey, it's hard!

896
00:36:23,371 --> 00:36:26,981
Do you think you got the basic drawing skills back?

897
00:36:27,251 --> 00:36:28,311
No!

898
00:36:29,051 --> 00:36:30,381
It's just hard.

899
00:36:31,951 --> 00:36:34,651
(Even though it's hard, he keeps moving his pencil.)

900
00:36:34,651 --> 00:36:36,621
- It's very exhausting. - I'm exhausted.

901
00:36:37,261 --> 00:36:39,761
(My entire body aches.)

902
00:36:40,561 --> 00:36:42,791
(Staggering)

903
00:36:43,801 --> 00:36:46,131
(Shall I stop here?)

904
00:36:46,501 --> 00:36:48,301
It looks cool.

905
00:36:49,171 --> 00:36:51,471
(How does Kian84's drawing look after 3 and a half hours of work?)

906
00:36:53,428 --> 00:36:55,088
(Let's see.)

907
00:36:56,388 --> 00:36:57,698
Why is it so light?

908
00:36:59,998 --> 00:37:01,928
- The cast is light in color. - Right?

909
00:37:02,128 --> 00:37:03,868
The drawing should be light.

910
00:37:04,238 --> 00:37:05,298
Right.

911
00:37:07,738 --> 00:37:10,308
(Now comes Chung Jae's drawing.)

912
00:37:10,308 --> 00:37:11,408
It's nice.

913
00:37:13,178 --> 00:37:15,308
(My goodness)

914
00:37:15,978 --> 00:37:17,978
He still has the basic skills.

915
00:37:18,518 --> 00:37:21,448
- Kian84's drawing is nice. - It's cool.

916
00:37:21,718 --> 00:37:23,458
They drew the same cast,

917
00:37:23,458 --> 00:37:25,158
- but the drawings are so different. - Their styles are different.

918
00:37:25,158 --> 00:37:27,088
- Their styles are different. - It's fun to see that.

919
00:37:27,228 --> 00:37:28,888
- They're really good. - Right.

920
00:37:31,628 --> 00:37:33,698
- I'll take this. - Okay.

921
00:37:34,928 --> 00:37:36,998
I want to get back to it when I have time.

922
00:37:39,168 --> 00:37:41,668
I hope you didn't get more stressed out at the studio.

923
00:37:41,868 --> 00:37:43,108
I didn't.

924
00:37:44,378 --> 00:37:47,548
I hadn't done it in years,

925
00:37:47,548 --> 00:37:49,808
so I'm proud that I could do it somehow.

926
00:37:50,648 --> 00:37:52,618
I guess you forget the techniques if you don't use them.

927
00:37:53,718 --> 00:37:57,118
(In a container, he puts the drawing he made after a long hiatus.)

928
00:37:58,688 --> 00:37:59,788
Will you keep on working?

929
00:38:00,288 --> 00:38:01,728
No. Because of this...

930
00:38:01,728 --> 00:38:03,698
(He can't work anymore.)

931
00:38:03,698 --> 00:38:05,228
I have no energy.

932
00:38:05,728 --> 00:38:06,798
- I'll need to go home. - Really?

933
00:38:07,128 --> 00:38:08,698
- Are you exhausted? - I'm exhausted.

934
00:38:08,698 --> 00:38:10,698
You should finish your work.

935
00:38:12,038 --> 00:38:14,308
(He averts his eyes.)

936
00:38:15,738 --> 00:38:19,108
(He plops down again after gathering his belongings.)

937
00:38:19,538 --> 00:38:21,208
(Yawning)

938
00:38:25,248 --> 00:38:26,348
All right.

939
00:38:27,018 --> 00:38:28,888
- Well done, Kian84. - Okay.

940
00:38:28,888 --> 00:38:30,558
- Good luck with your work. - Okay.

941
00:38:30,718 --> 00:38:32,988
- He's sending Kian84 away. - He wants Kian84 to leave.

942
00:38:32,988 --> 00:38:34,758
- He's pushing Kian84's back. - Seriously.

943
00:38:34,758 --> 00:38:36,158
"You should go, Kian84."

944
00:38:36,158 --> 00:38:38,028
- "You should go home." - "You should go home."

945
00:38:38,298 --> 00:38:40,828
- You should leave then. - That's right.

946
00:38:40,828 --> 00:38:42,968
You should go, "All right," and leave the studio.

947
00:38:43,568 --> 00:38:45,168
I don't want to go home when I meet Chung Jae.

948
00:38:45,968 --> 00:38:47,608
You must love him a lot.

949
00:38:47,938 --> 00:38:50,608
- "You must love him a lot." - Being with him puts me at ease.

950
00:38:51,078 --> 00:38:53,038
- In every age group... - There are such friends.

951
00:38:53,038 --> 00:38:54,108
- That's right. - That's right.

952
00:38:54,108 --> 00:38:55,948
- You want to spend time with them. - That's right.

953
00:38:56,648 --> 00:38:57,648
(My goodness)

954
00:38:58,178 --> 00:38:59,178
(Gosh)

955
00:38:59,848 --> 00:39:01,318
Please massage my neck.

956
00:39:02,918 --> 00:39:03,988
Harder.

957
00:39:04,088 --> 00:39:06,218
(It feels nice.)

958
00:39:07,528 --> 00:39:08,528
(Slapping)

959
00:39:09,958 --> 00:39:10,958
(Slapping)

960
00:39:10,958 --> 00:39:14,068
"All right. Let's go."

961
00:39:14,298 --> 00:39:16,368
Do you know what he'll do next if Kian84 still refuses to leave?

962
00:39:16,368 --> 00:39:17,998
He'll begin to turn off the lights.

963
00:39:18,738 --> 00:39:20,098
- That's right. - Then he'll leave with Kian84.

964
00:39:20,098 --> 00:39:21,868
- That's right. - Then he'll leave with Kian84.

965
00:39:21,868 --> 00:39:23,008
He'll begin to vacuum the floor.

966
00:39:23,008 --> 00:39:25,338
- That's right. - "I turned off the AC."

967
00:39:27,078 --> 00:39:28,878
You should turn off the AC first.

968
00:39:28,878 --> 00:39:30,178
"Let's close the door."

969
00:39:32,678 --> 00:39:33,848
Why did you finish it so quickly?

970
00:39:34,988 --> 00:39:37,618
Come to think of it, my hands are covered in pencil lead.

971
00:39:37,618 --> 00:39:38,658
Okay.

972
00:39:40,088 --> 00:39:42,858
(I'll put away the cast statue!)

973
00:39:42,858 --> 00:39:45,598
Look at that. He must have an appointment afterward.

974
00:39:45,758 --> 00:39:46,928
He's putting everything away.

975
00:39:48,528 --> 00:39:49,698
I'll get going.

976
00:39:50,338 --> 00:39:51,398
Okay.

977
00:39:51,398 --> 00:39:52,398
(Chuckling)

978
00:39:53,238 --> 00:39:54,408
I'll get going. Stay inside.

979
00:39:55,068 --> 00:39:56,338
- Bye, Kian84. - Okay.

980
00:39:58,238 --> 00:39:59,338
Bye.

981
00:40:00,038 --> 00:40:01,848
Kian84 is disappointed about...

982
00:40:01,848 --> 00:40:03,818
- going home. - He's disappointed.

983
00:40:03,818 --> 00:40:05,878
- Having beer would've been nice. - That's right.

984
00:40:05,948 --> 00:40:09,448
(He puts disappointment aside and hits the streets again.)

985
00:40:10,258 --> 00:40:11,858
- Hello. - Welcome.

986
00:40:21,828 --> 00:40:24,168
He's disappointed. There's disappointment on his face.

987
00:40:24,898 --> 00:40:26,368
It was good to see him again after a long while.

988
00:40:26,768 --> 00:40:27,908
That's right.

989
00:40:28,008 --> 00:40:29,338
You should meet up with Chimchakman.

990
00:40:29,338 --> 00:40:30,378
My goodness.

991
00:40:37,848 --> 00:40:39,048
Hello.

992
00:40:39,048 --> 00:40:41,718
(Grunting)

993
00:40:41,718 --> 00:40:43,988
- Is this a pansori singer? - What's up?

994
00:40:44,388 --> 00:40:45,958
Which slippers should I wear? Can I wear any pair?

995
00:40:45,958 --> 00:40:47,158
- Hold on. - Wear any pair.

996
00:40:47,258 --> 00:40:48,928
- Did you actually go to him? - He's Chimchakman!

997
00:40:50,298 --> 00:40:51,428
Chimchakman!

998
00:40:52,258 --> 00:40:54,128
His real name is Lee Byung Gun.

999
00:40:54,498 --> 00:40:57,238
His former name was Lee Mal Nyeon...

1000
00:40:57,638 --> 00:40:59,168
and his current name is Chimchakman.

1001
00:40:59,538 --> 00:41:02,008
(He was Kian84's roommate in the past.)

1002
00:41:02,008 --> 00:41:03,578
Here's what I think.

1003
00:41:03,938 --> 00:41:08,308
(He's Kian84's close friend who used to draw webtoons too.)

1004
00:41:09,078 --> 00:41:12,778
Now, he doesn't draw at all.

1005
00:41:13,248 --> 00:41:15,688
His job completely changed.

1006
00:41:16,018 --> 00:41:18,818
When I tell him my worries,

1007
00:41:19,088 --> 00:41:22,128
he gives me answers from a perspective...

1008
00:41:22,628 --> 00:41:24,528
I've never thought about.

1009
00:41:24,528 --> 00:41:26,098
His way of listening to my worries...

1010
00:41:26,558 --> 00:41:28,698
is different from Chung Jae's way.

1011
00:41:29,128 --> 00:41:30,868
Chung Jae is...

1012
00:41:30,868 --> 00:41:33,238
I go to the temple from time to time when I feel stressed out.

1013
00:41:33,368 --> 00:41:34,608
Just as if I visited the temple,

1014
00:41:35,108 --> 00:41:37,208
I visited Chung Jae.

1015
00:41:37,408 --> 00:41:39,638
As for Chimchakman,

1016
00:41:40,138 --> 00:41:42,348
I visited him as if I went to hang out at a comic book cafe.

1017
00:41:42,348 --> 00:41:43,618
I went to hang out with him.

1018
00:41:43,618 --> 00:41:45,018
(A place he goes to as if it's a comic book cafe)

1019
00:41:45,878 --> 00:41:47,048
Gosh.

1020
00:41:47,188 --> 00:41:49,048
- Aren't you busy? - Nice socks.

1021
00:41:49,048 --> 00:41:50,118
Aren't you busy?

1022
00:41:50,288 --> 00:41:52,358
I'm always busy.

1023
00:41:53,058 --> 00:41:54,058
Are you done with work?

1024
00:41:54,058 --> 00:41:55,528
Yes. I'm done with work.

1025
00:41:59,228 --> 00:42:00,728
- What's this? - This?

1026
00:42:00,868 --> 00:42:02,828
- A plaster figure? - I drew this today.

1027
00:42:03,498 --> 00:42:04,798
- Today? Why? - Yes.

1028
00:42:05,398 --> 00:42:07,008
I just felt like it.

1029
00:42:07,668 --> 00:42:09,168
Do you want to try drawing it as well?

1030
00:42:09,208 --> 00:42:10,378
I don't know how to.

1031
00:42:10,378 --> 00:42:11,738
You drew these to enter college before.

1032
00:42:11,808 --> 00:42:13,508
I'm awful at drawing them.

1033
00:42:14,978 --> 00:42:17,118
- Is this his office? - It's his office.

1034
00:42:17,278 --> 00:42:19,418
The place where he films for his content?

1035
00:42:19,978 --> 00:42:21,348
- It's fascinating, isn't it? - It's fascinating.

1036
00:42:21,348 --> 00:42:23,088
Right!

1037
00:42:23,088 --> 00:42:24,718
- I saw this place often. - Right.

1038
00:42:24,758 --> 00:42:26,518
So this is where he films for his content.

1039
00:42:28,728 --> 00:42:30,128
(Just like a comic book cafe,)

1040
00:42:30,128 --> 00:42:33,158
(the office is filled with cute items.)

1041
00:42:34,628 --> 00:42:36,298
- How much was this? - This?

1042
00:42:36,528 --> 00:42:37,638
They're a lot more expensive now.

1043
00:42:38,098 --> 00:42:39,498
They weren't even 30 dollars back then,

1044
00:42:39,498 --> 00:42:40,868
but they're around 70 dollars now.

1045
00:42:40,868 --> 00:42:42,168
- Really? - Yes.

1046
00:42:42,168 --> 00:42:43,468
- It looks nice, doesn't it? - Have a seat.

1047
00:42:44,038 --> 00:42:45,838
Why draw this all of a sudden?

1048
00:42:46,008 --> 00:42:47,478
- What? - Why draw this all of a sudden?

1049
00:42:48,348 --> 00:42:49,508
I draw these from time to time.

1050
00:42:49,978 --> 00:42:51,148
- Regularly? - Yes.

1051
00:42:52,148 --> 00:42:53,918
Didn't you just get this?

1052
00:42:54,418 --> 00:42:55,948
Did you draw it at an art school?

1053
00:42:55,948 --> 00:42:58,288
No. Chung Jae's studio is in Hanam.

1054
00:42:58,288 --> 00:42:59,718
I went there to draw it.

1055
00:43:00,988 --> 00:43:02,858
Their conversation consists of only short answers.

1056
00:43:04,598 --> 00:43:05,698
Do you want to draw with me?

1057
00:43:05,758 --> 00:43:06,998
No. I don't know how to.

1058
00:43:07,728 --> 00:43:09,128
It's just drawing how it looks.

1059
00:43:09,128 --> 00:43:11,068
I don't know how to draw these things.

1060
00:43:11,568 --> 00:43:14,068
The tone feels different from when talking with Chung Jae.

1061
00:43:14,068 --> 00:43:15,538
That's how it is.

1062
00:43:15,908 --> 00:43:17,508
Tones differ between friends.

1063
00:43:17,508 --> 00:43:19,508
- Right. - I didn't realize that at all.

1064
00:43:19,508 --> 00:43:20,608
It feels completely different.

1065
00:43:20,608 --> 00:43:22,278
"What?" It feels like this with him.

1066
00:43:22,278 --> 00:43:23,948
- You guys go back and forth. - You're right.

1067
00:43:25,218 --> 00:43:26,378
I heard you don't draw these days.

1068
00:43:26,378 --> 00:43:27,588
I don't draw now.

1069
00:43:28,448 --> 00:43:30,718
- You... - I heard you retired from drawing.

1070
00:43:31,088 --> 00:43:32,088
Right.

1071
00:43:32,288 --> 00:43:33,718
It was because...

1072
00:43:33,828 --> 00:43:35,528
people kept expecting things from me.

1073
00:43:36,028 --> 00:43:37,498
Who? No one expects things from you.

1074
00:43:37,498 --> 00:43:40,568
When I appear on a show or something like that,

1075
00:43:41,198 --> 00:43:42,428
they ask me if there's something I have drawn.

1076
00:43:42,498 --> 00:43:44,268
They ask if I can draw on the spot.

1077
00:43:44,368 --> 00:43:46,398
I get it.

1078
00:43:47,038 --> 00:43:48,908
That was why...

1079
00:43:49,138 --> 00:43:51,038
I was forced to announce my retirement from drawing.

1080
00:43:51,038 --> 00:43:52,478
People might think...

1081
00:43:52,538 --> 00:43:53,978
you're someone like Mr. Huh Young Man.

1082
00:43:53,978 --> 00:43:55,278
Why announce your retirement from drawing?

1083
00:43:55,278 --> 00:43:57,548
I would keep being asked to draw...

1084
00:43:57,548 --> 00:43:58,748
if I didn't do that.

1085
00:43:59,948 --> 00:44:01,088
Oh, I'll get you some coffee.

1086
00:44:01,088 --> 00:44:02,648
(I'll get you some coffee.)

1087
00:44:05,418 --> 00:44:06,488
Gosh.

1088
00:44:07,018 --> 00:44:08,988
Are your shows doing well?

1089
00:44:09,228 --> 00:44:10,458
- My shows? - Yes.

1090
00:44:10,858 --> 00:44:12,128
I'm just doing what I can.

1091
00:44:12,128 --> 00:44:14,828
- The coffee is in highball glasses. - Right.

1092
00:44:15,598 --> 00:44:17,338
You seem to be doing well.

1093
00:44:17,338 --> 00:44:18,398
What do you mean I'm doing well?

1094
00:44:18,398 --> 00:44:19,538
I'm not doing much.

1095
00:44:21,638 --> 00:44:23,338
I've been drawing these days.

1096
00:44:23,338 --> 00:44:25,038
I've been struggling though.

1097
00:44:25,208 --> 00:44:27,508
He feels like an elder at a real estate agency.

1098
00:44:27,678 --> 00:44:30,078
- Right. It feels comfortable. - A trendy real estate agency.

1099
00:44:30,448 --> 00:44:32,148
You've been drawing a lot.

1100
00:44:32,148 --> 00:44:34,418
There was a big pile of drawings when I visited you the other time.

1101
00:44:34,588 --> 00:44:36,688
I did draw a lot,

1102
00:44:37,758 --> 00:44:40,018
but around five of them...

1103
00:44:40,588 --> 00:44:41,628
seemed worthless.

1104
00:44:41,628 --> 00:44:42,928
- Five of them seemed worthless? - They weren't good enough.

1105
00:44:42,928 --> 00:44:45,258
- Out of how many? - I drew around 20.

1106
00:44:45,298 --> 00:44:47,528
Five of them seemed worthless.

1107
00:44:47,868 --> 00:44:50,068
I saw the ones that I completed...

1108
00:44:50,068 --> 00:44:51,368
- You didn't like them? - and I didn't like them.

1109
00:44:51,998 --> 00:44:54,468
It feels harder the more I try to draw.

1110
00:44:55,668 --> 00:44:58,908
Are you feeling stressed out these days...

1111
00:44:59,838 --> 00:45:02,578
because you aren't feeling satisfied from your drawing?

1112
00:45:03,178 --> 00:45:04,818
These days? Yes.

1113
00:45:05,718 --> 00:45:07,518
I don't know what I'm trying to do.

1114
00:45:08,448 --> 00:45:09,788
Being on shows and drawing...

1115
00:45:10,988 --> 00:45:14,428
were two of the main things I did.

1116
00:45:15,258 --> 00:45:16,758
Running...

1117
00:45:16,928 --> 00:45:19,328
suddenly became a part of my life.

1118
00:45:19,398 --> 00:45:21,098
- Right. - So...

1119
00:45:21,098 --> 00:45:22,828
I can't seem to focus on one thing.

1120
00:45:22,928 --> 00:45:25,138
I understand.

1121
00:45:25,368 --> 00:45:27,268
You can't do 20 things that you like all at once.

1122
00:45:27,268 --> 00:45:28,408
- Right. - Right.

1123
00:45:28,408 --> 00:45:29,938
- You have to be less ambitious. - Right.

1124
00:45:30,538 --> 00:45:33,178
I can't seem to focus on one thing.

1125
00:45:34,148 --> 00:45:35,708
Get it out of your life then.

1126
00:45:35,808 --> 00:45:37,108
- Get what out? - Running.

1127
00:45:37,278 --> 00:45:38,618
He's so straightforward.

1128
00:45:39,078 --> 00:45:40,818
- Get rid of running. - I can't get rid of running.

1129
00:45:40,818 --> 00:45:42,548
- Why not? - It's my main profession right now.

1130
00:45:42,548 --> 00:45:43,748
Then be on fewer shows.

1131
00:45:43,988 --> 00:45:45,258
- What? - Be on fewer shows.

1132
00:45:45,558 --> 00:45:46,558
- Be on fewer shows? - Yes.

1133
00:45:48,628 --> 00:45:51,998
What do you usually do during the week?

1134
00:45:52,798 --> 00:45:54,468
- Me? I run. - Yes.

1135
00:45:55,828 --> 00:45:56,998
- You run. - I run some more.

1136
00:45:56,998 --> 00:45:58,068
Gosh.

1137
00:45:58,598 --> 00:45:59,698
I draw.

1138
00:46:00,098 --> 00:46:01,138
I curse.

1139
00:46:01,368 --> 00:46:02,438
Right.

1140
00:46:02,438 --> 00:46:04,108
(I see.)

1141
00:46:04,208 --> 00:46:05,708
Do you enjoy running?

1142
00:46:05,708 --> 00:46:06,908
Yes. I enjoy running.

1143
00:46:07,008 --> 00:46:10,108
I'll be running around 35km tomorrow as well.

1144
00:46:10,878 --> 00:46:13,648
How is running 35km possible?

1145
00:46:14,288 --> 00:46:17,048
- That's over a half marathon. - Right?

1146
00:46:17,188 --> 00:46:19,288
How would you draw after running 35km?

1147
00:46:19,288 --> 00:46:20,288
- That won't be possible. - No.

1148
00:46:20,288 --> 00:46:21,528
You won't have the strength to pick up your brush.

1149
00:46:22,858 --> 00:46:25,198
I'm a water deer these days. I go crazy.

1150
00:46:25,198 --> 00:46:26,558
- It's all settled then. - What?

1151
00:46:26,558 --> 00:46:28,128
It's all settled if you enjoy it.

1152
00:46:28,498 --> 00:46:29,568
Is that so?

1153
00:46:32,598 --> 00:46:34,068
Has anything changed in your studio?

1154
00:46:35,768 --> 00:46:37,478
When was the last time you came?

1155
00:46:37,638 --> 00:46:39,338
I think it has been around a year.

1156
00:46:39,538 --> 00:46:41,648
You briefly visited the fourth floor, right?

1157
00:46:44,148 --> 00:46:46,848
You're still doing well after moving here.

1158
00:46:47,888 --> 00:46:49,918
(Walking around)

1159
00:46:50,758 --> 00:46:52,518
I like this type of drawing.

1160
00:46:52,518 --> 00:46:54,428
Back then,

1161
00:46:54,588 --> 00:46:56,128
I didn't think highly of it. I like it now.

1162
00:46:56,128 --> 00:46:57,998
- He didn't think highly of it? - Right.

1163
00:46:58,898 --> 00:47:00,358
I thought you were awful at drawing in the past.

1164
00:47:00,358 --> 00:47:02,128
But it looks impressive now.

1165
00:47:02,228 --> 00:47:03,398
"Impressive?"

1166
00:47:04,368 --> 00:47:07,908
We started with drawing comics, but we don't draw anymore.

1167
00:47:07,908 --> 00:47:08,938
Right.

1168
00:47:09,168 --> 00:47:10,808
You still draw these days though.

1169
00:47:10,838 --> 00:47:13,838
I thought I shouldn't be giving up on that.

1170
00:47:14,078 --> 00:47:15,308
It's still...

1171
00:47:15,308 --> 00:47:16,948
- Why does that matter? - something I started with.

1172
00:47:17,008 --> 00:47:19,278
What kind of angle is this?

1173
00:47:19,278 --> 00:47:20,478
It feels like an independent film.

1174
00:47:21,348 --> 00:47:22,688
In movies like "Decision to Leave,"

1175
00:47:22,688 --> 00:47:25,558
they stand like that when sharing delicate emotions.

1176
00:47:25,558 --> 00:47:26,988
(Scene 5: Sharing delicate emotions)

1177
00:47:26,988 --> 00:47:29,458
We didn't want to look at each other's faces for a long time.

1178
00:47:30,058 --> 00:47:32,158
- What? - But if I think about it,

1179
00:47:32,158 --> 00:47:34,498
I don't think I've looked into my friend's face when talking.

1180
00:47:34,498 --> 00:47:36,498
- It's weird to do that. - My male friends.

1181
00:47:36,498 --> 00:47:38,238
It will feel weird to do that with true friends.

1182
00:47:38,598 --> 00:47:40,768
We just need to be aware of where we are.

1183
00:47:41,568 --> 00:47:43,938
I thought I shouldn't be giving up on that.

1184
00:47:44,538 --> 00:47:45,808
It's still...

1185
00:47:45,808 --> 00:47:47,408
- Why does that matter? - something I started with.

1186
00:47:47,908 --> 00:47:50,578
"I started with drawing and I have the roots of drawing."

1187
00:47:50,578 --> 00:47:52,718
"I must not give up on drawing."

1188
00:47:53,578 --> 00:47:55,348
- There's no need to think that way. - Well,

1189
00:47:55,348 --> 00:47:57,988
that was something I got complimented for throughout my life.

1190
00:47:58,088 --> 00:48:00,358
You still get complimented for other things.

1191
00:48:00,488 --> 00:48:01,758
- But... - You're a great runner.

1192
00:48:01,758 --> 00:48:04,358
No. I won't get any compliments for my running.

1193
00:48:04,458 --> 00:48:05,458
I'm not that good.

1194
00:48:05,458 --> 00:48:07,928
- You don't need those compliments. - Others run over 80km.

1195
00:48:07,928 --> 00:48:10,068
- What? - Must you get complimented?

1196
00:48:10,098 --> 00:48:12,998
I still should get complimented for what I do.

1197
00:48:13,698 --> 00:48:17,008
I got really impressed by him while watching his shows.

1198
00:48:17,678 --> 00:48:20,308
"Who cares if you're not so good?" He seems to have this mindset.

1199
00:48:20,308 --> 00:48:21,878
Right. He was always like that.

1200
00:48:23,048 --> 00:48:24,948
This is what I think.

1201
00:48:26,148 --> 00:48:28,948
The acts that you do are impressive.

1202
00:48:29,188 --> 00:48:31,358
- You do extraordinary acts. - Right.

1203
00:48:32,058 --> 00:48:33,788
You should make them into a form of art...

1204
00:48:33,788 --> 00:48:35,528
and do performance art.

1205
00:48:36,288 --> 00:48:39,928
You tried sitting and drawing all day long,

1206
00:48:40,528 --> 00:48:41,798
but you didn't get what you wanted, right?

1207
00:48:42,368 --> 00:48:44,638
You like moving around, don't you?

1208
00:48:44,668 --> 00:48:47,268
Apply paint to your feet and run!

1209
00:48:47,768 --> 00:48:50,338
- That's what art is. - Right.

1210
00:48:50,438 --> 00:48:53,178
Mix up the things that you like.

1211
00:48:53,608 --> 00:48:56,648
Why use the brush and your feet separately?

1212
00:48:56,648 --> 00:48:57,678
Use them together.

1213
00:48:57,718 --> 00:48:59,978
- But... - You're great with your feet.

1214
00:48:59,978 --> 00:49:01,548
That would make me seem like too much of an attention seeker.

1215
00:49:01,548 --> 00:49:02,718
- No. - People would think that way.

1216
00:49:03,018 --> 00:49:04,758
- I think it's a good idea. - What?

1217
00:49:04,758 --> 00:49:05,758
It's a good idea.

1218
00:49:05,818 --> 00:49:07,958
He always comes up with a solution.

1219
00:49:07,958 --> 00:49:10,388
When I tell him I don't want to age, he tells me to become a cyborg.

1220
00:49:11,628 --> 00:49:12,958
- That's a clear solution. - Right.

1221
00:49:13,728 --> 00:49:15,098
- He's a great friend. - That's great.

1222
00:49:15,098 --> 00:49:16,798
He's full of ideas as he's a cartoonist.

1223
00:49:16,798 --> 00:49:19,098
- It's best to speak nonsense. - Right.

1224
00:49:19,198 --> 00:49:20,468
- It helps relieve stress. - Right.

1225
00:49:21,768 --> 00:49:22,908
What else is there?

1226
00:49:22,908 --> 00:49:24,508
- There's the fourth floor. - Right.

1227
00:49:25,538 --> 00:49:26,538
(Let's go up.)

1228
00:49:26,538 --> 00:49:28,178
I don't think it will be a bad idea to do that.

1229
00:49:32,978 --> 00:49:34,618
It's just the same. It's nice.

1230
00:49:35,118 --> 00:49:36,788
This was when you were on YouTube.

1231
00:49:36,788 --> 00:49:37,788
Right.

1232
00:49:39,388 --> 00:49:40,688
Your drawing is here as well.

1233
00:49:40,988 --> 00:49:42,588
- Where? Right. - You drew this.

1234
00:49:44,046 --> 00:49:45,346
- What is that? - Gosh.

1235
00:49:45,371 --> 00:49:47,111
(Kian84's masterpiece in Chimchakman's office?)

1236
00:49:47,987 --> 00:49:49,387
Your drawing is here as well.

1237
00:49:49,557 --> 00:49:51,227
- Where? Right. - You drew this.

1238
00:49:51,227 --> 00:49:52,327
(Kian84 is doing a tour around Chimchakman's office.)

1239
00:49:52,357 --> 00:49:53,427
It's still here.

1240
00:49:53,867 --> 00:49:55,097
- What is that? - Gosh.

1241
00:49:56,127 --> 00:49:57,667
- Right. - "Chimchakman's Future."

1242
00:49:58,237 --> 00:50:00,337
It was drawn in 2024. It has been a year.

1243
00:50:00,337 --> 00:50:01,337
This one.

1244
00:50:04,607 --> 00:50:06,777
All of my employees left except for one.

1245
00:50:07,277 --> 00:50:08,607
- Really? - There's one left in the office.

1246
00:50:08,877 --> 00:50:11,117
The two who were in the office...

1247
00:50:11,117 --> 00:50:13,347
are working on their own now.

1248
00:50:13,347 --> 00:50:14,917
- They became independent. - That's great.

1249
00:50:14,917 --> 00:50:16,247
It's like they've finished their training.

1250
00:50:16,247 --> 00:50:17,587
So that's what they do.

1251
00:50:17,617 --> 00:50:19,287
Like how chefs become independent and run their own restaurants.

1252
00:50:19,287 --> 00:50:20,387
Right.

1253
00:50:20,387 --> 00:50:23,527
I visited them, and they were working hard on their own.

1254
00:50:23,957 --> 00:50:26,697
It would be great when they visit you after becoming successful.

1255
00:50:26,697 --> 00:50:28,627
I would love it for them to be successful.

1256
00:50:28,627 --> 00:50:30,027
- Definitely. - Right.

1257
00:50:30,027 --> 00:50:31,397
- I'm rooting for them. - Right.

1258
00:50:31,697 --> 00:50:34,107
You must have a lot to work on.

1259
00:50:34,307 --> 00:50:36,307
- Well... - Why do you only have one employee?

1260
00:50:37,037 --> 00:50:40,407
When my employees help me out,

1261
00:50:40,877 --> 00:50:42,377
- I fix all of their work. - Right.

1262
00:50:42,377 --> 00:50:43,577
That takes up more time.

1263
00:50:44,277 --> 00:50:45,577
You were always like that.

1264
00:50:46,277 --> 00:50:47,817
- I just... - You did all the work.

1265
00:50:47,817 --> 00:50:51,417
don't know how to work as a team with others.

1266
00:50:51,617 --> 00:50:55,527
I'm not like one of those global masters.

1267
00:50:56,157 --> 00:50:59,057
I heard those people had 200 to 300 assistants.

1268
00:51:00,367 --> 00:51:02,367
I'm not someone who is at that level.

1269
00:51:02,367 --> 00:51:06,397
The number of assistants isn't what's important.

1270
00:51:06,397 --> 00:51:07,407
Right.

1271
00:51:07,407 --> 00:51:11,007
If you want someone to work just the way you want them to work,

1272
00:51:11,377 --> 00:51:13,437
you must invest a lot of time to that person.

1273
00:51:13,437 --> 00:51:14,747
- He sounds so mature. - Right.

1274
00:51:14,977 --> 00:51:17,417
But you don't tend to do that.

1275
00:51:17,847 --> 00:51:19,817
It takes a lot more time to match each other.

1276
00:51:19,817 --> 00:51:21,517
That is why you need to invest a lot of time...

1277
00:51:21,517 --> 00:51:23,487
- to the people you work with. - Right.

1278
00:51:23,487 --> 00:51:25,057
You need to reduce the time of your running sessions.

1279
00:51:25,517 --> 00:51:26,617
- It's the same solution. - It's the same solution.

1280
00:51:26,617 --> 00:51:28,727
- It always ends there. - "Run less."

1281
00:51:29,127 --> 00:51:30,397
Run less...

1282
00:51:30,397 --> 00:51:34,527
and get into sync with the one who draws with you.

1283
00:51:34,967 --> 00:51:36,597
- Right. - You have to do that...

1284
00:51:36,697 --> 00:51:40,197
to be able to work with 1 or 2 people.

1285
00:51:40,197 --> 00:51:41,207
Right.

1286
00:51:41,207 --> 00:51:44,337
It's the same process for 200 to 300 people.

1287
00:51:45,007 --> 00:51:46,777
You were like that when you worked on webtoons.

1288
00:51:47,347 --> 00:51:48,377
Right.

1289
00:51:48,377 --> 00:51:49,647
You ended up doing everything.

1290
00:51:50,777 --> 00:51:55,247
Your assistant drew a part for you,

1291
00:51:55,247 --> 00:51:57,757
but you sat there and fixed everything.

1292
00:51:57,817 --> 00:51:58,987
But back then...

1293
00:51:58,987 --> 00:52:00,687
You just moved around to do all of the drawing.

1294
00:52:00,687 --> 00:52:01,827
But back then...

1295
00:52:02,727 --> 00:52:03,727
- He moved around a lot. - Gosh.

1296
00:52:03,727 --> 00:52:04,997
- He's right. - He sounds so cool-headed.

1297
00:52:05,797 --> 00:52:07,467
There was a lot more to work with when working with the others.

1298
00:52:07,527 --> 00:52:11,137
I just needed to add the details for their webtoon work.

1299
00:52:11,297 --> 00:52:12,797
But for drawing, it's different.

1300
00:52:12,797 --> 00:52:13,837
It's harder...

1301
00:52:15,067 --> 00:52:16,737
with drawing.

1302
00:52:17,707 --> 00:52:21,547
It's hard to find the perfect person.

1303
00:52:21,547 --> 00:52:22,547
It isn't easy to do that.

1304
00:52:22,547 --> 00:52:23,747
(A serious conversation in slippers)

1305
00:52:23,917 --> 00:52:25,547
I'm not here to share my struggles with you.

1306
00:52:25,777 --> 00:52:28,647
Just apply paint to your feet.

1307
00:52:29,987 --> 00:52:31,017
Do that.

1308
00:52:31,887 --> 00:52:32,987
You can run for a long time.

1309
00:52:32,987 --> 00:52:34,127
- What? - You can run for a long time.

1310
00:52:34,127 --> 00:52:36,527
- I'm not that great of a runner. - It's a different spot to run.

1311
00:52:36,527 --> 00:52:38,957
- I'm not a great runner. - You can run on top of paper.

1312
00:52:38,957 --> 00:52:40,427
On top of paper?

1313
00:52:40,567 --> 00:52:42,697
Whether it be 10km or 20km long.

1314
00:52:42,697 --> 00:52:44,437
I think it will seem like a work of art.

1315
00:52:45,167 --> 00:52:47,967
- Thank you for giving me advice. - You seemed frustrated.

1316
00:52:48,237 --> 00:52:49,507
- What? - You seemed frustrated.

1317
00:52:49,507 --> 00:52:51,277
I always get frustrated.

1318
00:52:51,707 --> 00:52:53,807
He's a great person to talk with.

1319
00:52:54,007 --> 00:52:55,507
- He's very calm. - Right.

1320
00:52:56,077 --> 00:52:57,877
- Should we play Starcraft? - What?

1321
00:52:59,117 --> 00:53:01,047
(A video game battle after a while)

1322
00:53:01,047 --> 00:53:02,317
- This is the best thing to do. - This is fun.

1323
00:53:02,317 --> 00:53:03,417
It seems so comfortable.

1324
00:53:03,887 --> 00:53:05,517
(Totally immersed)

1325
00:53:05,657 --> 00:53:07,327
(Doing his best)

1326
00:53:11,627 --> 00:53:13,327
(Laughing)

1327
00:53:13,727 --> 00:53:14,767
This is fun.

1328
00:53:16,767 --> 00:53:17,997
You're finally laughing.

1329
00:53:20,337 --> 00:53:22,267
- You're finally laughing. - This is so much fun.

1330
00:53:23,337 --> 00:53:24,777
Those simple things are the best.

1331
00:53:24,777 --> 00:53:26,007
It's fun to do the things you did in the past.

1332
00:53:26,007 --> 00:53:27,007
Right.

1333
00:53:27,007 --> 00:53:28,507
There's a different therapeutic vibe at this place...

1334
00:53:28,507 --> 00:53:30,277
- from Chung Jae's place. - Right.

1335
00:53:31,477 --> 00:53:33,617
(Smiling)

1336
00:53:33,617 --> 00:53:35,017
Gosh, it's been a while since I played.

1337
00:53:37,357 --> 00:53:38,557
This is what I think.

1338
00:53:39,287 --> 00:53:41,157
When I first started working on webtoons,

1339
00:53:41,557 --> 00:53:43,427
I felt excited after waking up in the morning.

1340
00:53:43,787 --> 00:53:45,357
"I can express these things today."

1341
00:53:46,627 --> 00:53:48,867
It felt exhausting after working on them for a long time.

1342
00:53:49,467 --> 00:53:52,467
I think it's the same with the drawing I'm working on now.

1343
00:53:52,467 --> 00:53:54,737
I don't feel satisfied with my work...

1344
00:53:54,737 --> 00:53:57,337
and it feels exhausting.

1345
00:53:57,337 --> 00:53:58,807
I'm going through that process.

1346
00:53:59,407 --> 00:54:01,007
But whenever I feel that way,

1347
00:54:01,747 --> 00:54:04,377
I can talk to my fellow artists...

1348
00:54:04,817 --> 00:54:07,187
and it clears up my thoughts.

1349
00:54:07,517 --> 00:54:09,547
This is my way of overcoming those struggles.

1350
00:54:11,757 --> 00:54:12,917
Why are you here already?

1351
00:54:13,617 --> 00:54:15,127
- Hold on! - Hee Min!

1352
00:54:16,787 --> 00:54:17,897
Gosh.

1353
00:54:17,897 --> 00:54:19,157
(We can't see each other often like before,)

1354
00:54:19,527 --> 00:54:20,527
Gosh.

1355
00:54:20,527 --> 00:54:21,727
(but we always feel comfortable whenever we meet.)

1356
00:54:21,727 --> 00:54:22,927
You're good.

1357
00:54:23,627 --> 00:54:25,737
- I can beat you in the next one. - Should we play another round?

1358
00:54:25,997 --> 00:54:27,207
- What? - Should we play another round?

1359
00:54:28,107 --> 00:54:29,607
(Okay! I've Made up My Mind!)

1360
00:54:29,607 --> 00:54:31,507
- What? - What could it be about?

1361
00:54:36,847 --> 00:54:38,417
(Fast asleep)

1362
00:54:38,417 --> 00:54:39,417
Everything is white.

1363
00:54:39,417 --> 00:54:40,647
That must be a great place to sleep in.

1364
00:54:40,647 --> 00:54:42,087
You have a nice couch.

1365
00:54:42,247 --> 00:54:44,057
I've been sleeping on the couch for three months now.

1366
00:54:47,187 --> 00:54:48,187
(Walking)

1367
00:54:48,187 --> 00:54:49,227
Gosh.

1368
00:54:50,797 --> 00:54:52,597
- How cute. - How cute.

1369
00:55:02,367 --> 00:55:04,337
(Getting up)

1370
00:55:05,337 --> 00:55:06,537
Gosh.

1371
00:55:08,447 --> 00:55:09,847
(He heads straight into his dressing room.)

1372
00:55:09,847 --> 00:55:11,217
He has so many clothes.

1373
00:55:11,217 --> 00:55:13,177
I'm sure he doesn't wear all of them.

1374
00:55:13,847 --> 00:55:15,717
- Are you asking for some clothes? - It seems he is.

1375
00:55:16,187 --> 00:55:17,787
What are you getting on to?

1376
00:55:17,987 --> 00:55:19,457
I'm the first one on the list to get CODE KUNST's clothes.

1377
00:55:19,457 --> 00:55:20,457
(Securing his place)

1378
00:55:24,897 --> 00:55:26,757
He must be leaving right away without washing up.

1379
00:55:26,757 --> 00:55:28,097
(He gets changed to head outside while being half asleep.)

1380
00:55:33,067 --> 00:55:35,237
(He picks up his phone that is still ringing.)

1381
00:55:37,507 --> 00:55:39,577
(He leaves the house 10 minutes after waking up.)

1382
00:55:39,577 --> 00:55:40,907
Where could he be going?

1383
00:55:43,947 --> 00:55:45,777
(Walking)

1384
00:55:45,777 --> 00:55:47,017
He must be out for a walk.

1385
00:55:48,647 --> 00:55:49,747
(He follows the trail.)

1386
00:55:49,747 --> 00:55:50,787
The weather is great.

1387
00:55:53,187 --> 00:55:55,257
I should keep checking the time.

1388
00:55:55,727 --> 00:55:56,857
What's going on?

1389
00:55:57,397 --> 00:55:58,927
What? I don't have much time left!

1390
00:55:59,827 --> 00:56:00,967
I have to hurry!

1391
00:56:02,097 --> 00:56:04,367
- Emergency! - What's going on?

1392
00:56:04,567 --> 00:56:06,997
(He moves quickly as he seems to be short on time.)

1393
00:56:08,107 --> 00:56:09,167
Is it an opening rush?

1394
00:56:09,507 --> 00:56:11,637
He isn't someone who would do opening rushes.

1395
00:56:14,877 --> 00:56:16,977
- Hello. - Welcome.

1396
00:56:18,177 --> 00:56:19,847
- Welcome. - All right.

1397
00:56:20,047 --> 00:56:21,787
- You're here. - Right.

1398
00:56:22,287 --> 00:56:23,847
Shall I start shopping?

1399
00:56:24,187 --> 00:56:25,187
I see.

1400
00:56:26,387 --> 00:56:27,757
Is someone visiting?

1401
00:56:27,757 --> 00:56:29,587
It seems too early for that.

1402
00:56:31,727 --> 00:56:33,897
What was I going to buy?

1403
00:56:35,697 --> 00:56:37,127
(Picking up)

1404
00:56:37,597 --> 00:56:39,437
(Let's see.)

1405
00:56:40,297 --> 00:56:41,337
Gosh.

1406
00:56:41,337 --> 00:56:42,967
(He made a shopping list on his phone.)

1407
00:56:45,007 --> 00:56:46,077
Eggs.

1408
00:56:46,177 --> 00:56:48,007
There's a list for the things that are needed.

1409
00:56:48,007 --> 00:56:49,007
Right.

1410
00:56:49,877 --> 00:56:50,947
Hello?

1411
00:56:51,217 --> 00:56:53,447
(I don't know what to do.)

1412
00:56:54,047 --> 00:56:55,317
(He called Jang Woo to ask about the things to buy...)

1413
00:56:55,317 --> 00:56:56,687
(as he felt lost.)

1414
00:56:57,047 --> 00:56:58,157
What else was there?

1415
00:56:58,557 --> 00:56:59,857
How nice is that?

1416
00:57:00,927 --> 00:57:02,057
The protein drink.

1417
00:57:03,727 --> 00:57:05,857
(It heads straight into the basket.)

1418
00:57:06,727 --> 00:57:10,397
(He gets the things on the list without hesitation.)

1419
00:57:11,437 --> 00:57:13,297
It won't fit in the basket.

1420
00:57:14,707 --> 00:57:16,107
Will this be enough?

1421
00:57:18,377 --> 00:57:19,377
(Packed)

1422
00:57:19,377 --> 00:57:21,177
- There are that many things? - Is he having a snack party?

1423
00:57:22,107 --> 00:57:24,077
Okay. There we go.

1424
00:57:24,977 --> 00:57:26,577
- Thank you. - Thank you.

1425
00:57:26,577 --> 00:57:28,117
- See you. - I'll come again.

1426
00:57:28,117 --> 00:57:29,647
Okay. Goodbye.

1427
00:57:31,487 --> 00:57:33,787
I should take around five minutes until I get back home.

1428
00:57:35,357 --> 00:57:37,257
I have enough time. It's 11:57am.

1429
00:57:37,657 --> 00:57:40,297
Why is he constantly checking the time when he just went shopping?

1430
00:57:40,297 --> 00:57:41,497
(He keeps checking the time while heading back home.)

1431
00:57:44,497 --> 00:57:45,537
Siru.

1432
00:57:45,537 --> 00:57:47,037
(I'm back, Siru.)

1433
00:57:47,937 --> 00:57:49,067
Let's go.

1434
00:57:51,537 --> 00:57:55,307
(He places the things he bought in the kitchen.)

1435
00:57:56,147 --> 00:57:57,647
I should get changed first.

1436
00:57:59,717 --> 00:58:02,487
(He returns after getting changed into comfortable clothes.)

1437
00:58:04,317 --> 00:58:05,357
All right.

1438
00:58:08,657 --> 00:58:10,487
- I'm done with that. - What's going on?

1439
00:58:11,457 --> 00:58:13,597
I'm done with that and that.

1440
00:58:13,727 --> 00:58:14,827
What is that?

1441
00:58:16,867 --> 00:58:19,667
It's my schedule for the day.

1442
00:58:19,667 --> 00:58:22,507
It's just for that exact day.

1443
00:58:23,067 --> 00:58:25,237
While being under more control,

1444
00:58:25,637 --> 00:58:29,207
I thought that I would spend the day without making mistakes.

1445
00:58:29,207 --> 00:58:30,307
That was why I gave it a try.

1446
00:58:30,307 --> 00:58:32,177
You wanted to try living like a planner?

1447
00:58:32,177 --> 00:58:33,777
(You wanted to try living like a planner?)

1448
00:58:34,017 --> 00:58:37,517
I was planning to get my album done within the year.

1449
00:58:37,587 --> 00:58:40,087
An album takes around two years to make.

1450
00:58:40,087 --> 00:58:43,527
It takes around two years on average for an album.

1451
00:58:43,987 --> 00:58:45,727
But it's already my third year to work on the album.

1452
00:58:46,057 --> 00:58:48,397
This was when I started working on the album.

1453
00:58:48,527 --> 00:58:49,867
It was when Kwang Kyu visited.

1454
00:58:49,867 --> 00:58:51,437
(Around 2 years ago)

1455
00:58:53,267 --> 00:58:54,767
(Happy to see him)

1456
00:58:55,407 --> 00:58:59,777
I stopped at around 80 to 90 percent with each song.

1457
00:58:59,777 --> 00:59:03,377
I kept writing new songs at that point.

1458
00:59:03,777 --> 00:59:06,147
I ended up writing about 160 songs like that.

1459
00:59:06,747 --> 00:59:09,847
I thought that I wasn't going to finish the album within the year.

1460
00:59:10,217 --> 00:59:12,017
When I was on "Show Me the Money,"

1461
00:59:12,017 --> 00:59:14,427
I wrote 12 songs in 3 months.

1462
00:59:14,427 --> 00:59:15,427
- Right. - Right.

1463
00:59:15,427 --> 00:59:17,857
There was a deadline every week.

1464
00:59:17,857 --> 00:59:19,427
- Right. - There's this saying.

1465
00:59:19,427 --> 00:59:21,627
"The best source of inspiration is the deadline."

1466
00:59:22,267 --> 00:59:23,267
Right.

1467
00:59:23,267 --> 00:59:25,867
We had to come up with comedy skits for the very next day.

1468
00:59:26,097 --> 00:59:27,267
We did it with ease.

1469
00:59:27,267 --> 00:59:30,267
I did more than 50 percent of the work on deadline day.

1470
00:59:30,267 --> 00:59:31,607
I came with all of the storyline on that day.

1471
00:59:31,837 --> 00:59:35,847
I set deadlines with the schedule.

1472
00:59:36,277 --> 00:59:37,447
Actually,

1473
00:59:37,817 --> 00:59:40,417
I considered myself to be a great planless person.

1474
00:59:40,887 --> 00:59:43,147
I was someone at the peak...

1475
00:59:43,147 --> 00:59:45,417
of being planless.

1476
00:59:46,687 --> 00:59:48,257
(While eating a banana,)

1477
00:59:48,257 --> 00:59:50,627
(he threw the table tennis ball.)

1478
00:59:51,027 --> 00:59:52,657
(While organizing his clothes,)

1479
00:59:52,657 --> 00:59:54,967
(he lied on top of them.)

1480
00:59:54,967 --> 00:59:56,397
He was spontaneous.

1481
00:59:56,867 --> 00:59:58,737
I was like an animal in the zoo.

1482
00:59:58,737 --> 01:00:00,637
I did everything...

1483
01:00:00,637 --> 01:00:02,907
that caught my eye.

1484
01:00:04,107 --> 01:00:05,277
(Planless person at the peak)

1485
01:00:05,277 --> 01:00:06,777
He seemed to be free.

1486
01:00:07,337 --> 01:00:11,217
Ki Bum is the only real planner among all of us.

1487
01:00:11,217 --> 01:00:12,447
- Right. - Out of us.

1488
01:00:12,577 --> 01:00:14,317
- I'm right in the middle. - Right.

1489
01:00:14,317 --> 01:00:17,287
- You go back and forth. - Right.

1490
01:00:17,417 --> 01:00:19,787
I'm more of a planner.

1491
01:00:19,917 --> 01:00:20,987
- Who? - What?

1492
01:00:20,987 --> 01:00:22,257
- You? - Really?

1493
01:00:22,257 --> 01:00:24,027
You come here when it's hot.

1494
01:00:24,257 --> 01:00:25,357
All of that is planned.

1495
01:00:25,357 --> 01:00:26,457
- Really? - Gosh.

1496
01:00:26,457 --> 01:00:27,457
- Do you control the weather? - It's all planned.

1497
01:00:27,457 --> 01:00:29,627
- I consider everything. - You know when it'll be hot?

1498
01:00:29,767 --> 01:00:30,767
Of course.

1499
01:00:30,767 --> 01:00:33,397
I'm a hardcore planner when I travel.

1500
01:00:33,537 --> 01:00:35,007
I type everything out...

1501
01:00:35,007 --> 01:00:37,407
- and print 2 to 3 pages. - Really?

1502
01:00:37,407 --> 01:00:38,537
- For real? - I give them out.

1503
01:00:38,537 --> 01:00:39,737
- Oh, my. - To people you travel with?

1504
01:00:39,807 --> 01:00:41,177
When I travel...

1505
01:00:41,177 --> 01:00:42,677
Let's say the plane leaves at 1pm.

1506
01:00:42,947 --> 01:00:45,547
Then I'd pack at 10am that day.

1507
01:00:46,217 --> 01:00:47,987
What? The very day?

1508
01:00:47,987 --> 01:00:49,447
- I do that too. - Right?

1509
01:00:49,447 --> 01:00:51,157
I pack on the day I travel.

1510
01:00:51,317 --> 01:00:54,117
Because I studied abroad for many years,

1511
01:00:54,527 --> 01:00:56,127
I can pack quickly and leave.

1512
01:00:56,127 --> 01:00:58,697
- You're way below me. - Why?

1513
01:00:58,897 --> 01:01:00,227
I buy clothes at my destination.

1514
01:01:00,227 --> 01:01:01,297
(Why prepare in advance?)

1515
01:01:02,497 --> 01:01:03,997
- That's... - I travel with one outfit.

1516
01:01:03,997 --> 01:01:05,367
- Me too. - I don't bring anything.

1517
01:01:05,367 --> 01:01:07,437
- Toothbrush, toothpaste, clothes. - That's right.

1518
01:01:07,437 --> 01:01:09,637
- You're so romantic. - I just leave.

1519
01:01:09,737 --> 01:01:11,237
- That'd drive me crazy. - That's right.

1520
01:01:11,507 --> 01:01:12,577
- I'd just dress sloppily. - Socks.

1521
01:01:12,577 --> 01:01:13,607
I just need to cover myself.

1522
01:01:13,607 --> 01:01:15,077
(Spontaneity versus planning, a conflict of opinions)

1523
01:01:15,177 --> 01:01:18,377
Everything will be planned today.

1524
01:01:18,547 --> 01:01:19,617
Just everything.

1525
01:01:19,847 --> 01:01:21,747
Today, I'll become a planner.

1526
01:01:21,747 --> 01:01:23,087
(He becomes PLAN KUNST to complete his album.)

1527
01:01:23,087 --> 01:01:24,717
I'm so disappointed in you.

1528
01:01:24,717 --> 01:01:26,017
(You betrayer)

1529
01:01:27,117 --> 01:01:28,727
(11am, wake up)

1530
01:01:28,727 --> 01:01:31,327
That's why you keep timing everything.

1531
01:01:31,327 --> 01:01:32,597
(11am to 12pm, grocery shopping)

1532
01:01:32,597 --> 01:01:33,627
What else?

1533
01:01:33,627 --> 01:01:34,827
(Bacon, eggs, milk, banana, bread, fruits, loaf of bread)

1534
01:01:34,927 --> 01:01:36,827
(12pm to 12:30pm, walk home)

1535
01:01:36,827 --> 01:01:37,937
You're very planned.

1536
01:01:39,137 --> 01:01:40,237
That's done.

1537
01:01:40,567 --> 01:01:42,007
Gosh, this isn't hard.

1538
01:01:45,437 --> 01:01:47,677
(He checks the time again.)

1539
01:01:48,777 --> 01:01:50,247
(It's 12:19pm.)

1540
01:01:51,017 --> 01:01:52,017
(12pm to 12:30pm, walk home)

1541
01:01:52,017 --> 01:01:54,447
In the spare time...

1542
01:01:56,487 --> 01:01:58,457
(Thinking)

1543
01:01:59,257 --> 01:02:00,417
I should do things like this.

1544
01:02:01,887 --> 01:02:05,557
(Using his spare time to feed Siru and Panda)

1545
01:02:07,297 --> 01:02:09,097
I'm going to rest until 12:30pm.

1546
01:02:09,727 --> 01:02:12,297
I'd do the next task in advance if I have spare time.

1547
01:02:12,697 --> 01:02:13,737
Oh, really?

1548
01:02:13,867 --> 01:02:16,237
If I can't do the next task in advance, I can't help it,

1549
01:02:16,237 --> 01:02:17,607
but if I can, I would too.

1550
01:02:19,307 --> 01:02:20,437
Come here.

1551
01:02:20,437 --> 01:02:21,677
(Lying down)

1552
01:02:23,907 --> 01:02:25,177
(A relaxing time with Siru and Panda)

1553
01:02:25,177 --> 01:02:26,317
They're cute.

1554
01:02:26,477 --> 01:02:27,617
Gosh.

1555
01:02:27,817 --> 01:02:29,717
How adorable.

1556
01:02:29,717 --> 01:02:31,217
They're so dog-like.

1557
01:02:31,987 --> 01:02:33,757
(Jump)

1558
01:02:38,297 --> 01:02:40,757
(Getting up right away at the sound of the alarm)

1559
01:02:42,167 --> 01:02:44,397
- Oh, you set an alarm... - Okay.

1560
01:02:44,397 --> 01:02:46,897
- for every task you have. - That's right.

1561
01:02:47,167 --> 01:02:50,807
The cool guy's next task is...

1562
01:02:50,807 --> 01:02:51,837
(Let's see.)

1563
01:02:51,837 --> 01:02:53,237
The cool guy's next task.

1564
01:02:54,947 --> 01:02:56,107
Organizing the fridge.

1565
01:02:56,577 --> 01:02:57,917
Organizing the fridge?

1566
01:02:57,917 --> 01:02:59,447
- You could've done that in advance. - I know.

1567
01:02:59,447 --> 01:03:01,187
- That could've been done. - That's doable.

1568
01:03:01,347 --> 01:03:04,557
- But a first-time planner... - That's a very tight schedule.

1569
01:03:04,557 --> 01:03:06,487
shouldn't move up the task.

1570
01:03:06,487 --> 01:03:08,157
- In case you ruin the rest? - You were worried it'd be ruined.

1571
01:03:09,187 --> 01:03:10,787
(Opening the fridge, following his planner)

1572
01:03:10,787 --> 01:03:12,357
I got a new fridge.

1573
01:03:12,357 --> 01:03:14,767
My brother went to the US to study,

1574
01:03:15,127 --> 01:03:16,967
so he gave me the fridge he had.

1575
01:03:18,297 --> 01:03:20,397
He really eats little.

1576
01:03:20,837 --> 01:03:23,207
Yes. Now it feels like a real home.

1577
01:03:25,137 --> 01:03:26,307
There's nothing much, though.

1578
01:03:27,637 --> 01:03:29,407
Call me if you need to clean out the food.

1579
01:03:30,447 --> 01:03:33,017
You want to take that little of food I have?

1580
01:03:34,447 --> 01:03:37,147
The types of food that I can save time...

1581
01:03:37,147 --> 01:03:39,257
and quickly be done with.

1582
01:03:39,487 --> 01:03:40,987
I wanted to make that.

1583
01:03:40,987 --> 01:03:42,057
(He planned to organize the fridge to make it more time efficient.)

1584
01:03:42,827 --> 01:03:44,287
(It's 12:30pm.)

1585
01:03:46,097 --> 01:03:47,127
The drinks...

1586
01:03:47,127 --> 01:03:48,367
"So they can be taken out right away."

1587
01:03:48,367 --> 01:03:50,867
I drink them most often, so I'll put it on the topmost shelf.

1588
01:03:54,267 --> 01:03:55,407
Mom's side dishes.

1589
01:03:55,407 --> 01:03:57,207
(Following through his rules)

1590
01:03:57,207 --> 01:03:58,437
I'll put them here.

1591
01:03:59,007 --> 01:04:01,207
(Beeping)

1592
01:04:02,677 --> 01:04:04,617
That's what spontaneous people hear the most.

1593
01:04:04,617 --> 01:04:05,947
The beep.

1594
01:04:05,947 --> 01:04:07,847
That's right. I hear that a lot too.

1595
01:04:09,547 --> 01:04:12,017
(With the familiar sound, he quickly puts away his grocery.)

1596
01:04:12,017 --> 01:04:13,087
Olives.

1597
01:04:14,887 --> 01:04:17,487
Do I keep the bread in the freezer?

1598
01:04:20,257 --> 01:04:22,867
Since this is also scheduled, you need to move quickly.

1599
01:04:22,867 --> 01:04:26,697
Stir-fried kimchi, garlic, dried laver.

1600
01:04:27,197 --> 01:04:29,167
How should I put away eggs?

1601
01:04:31,977 --> 01:04:32,977
What?

1602
01:04:35,277 --> 01:04:37,477
- Oh, dear. - Oh, no. Time's up.

1603
01:04:37,547 --> 01:04:38,717
- What time is it? - It keeps ringing.

1604
01:04:38,717 --> 01:04:39,777
I feel so anxious.

1605
01:04:41,417 --> 01:04:42,747
(12:30pm to 1pm, return home and organize the fridge)

1606
01:04:42,747 --> 01:04:43,817
Wait.

1607
01:04:44,987 --> 01:04:47,317
(It's 1pm now. The time's up for his task.)

1608
01:04:49,157 --> 01:04:51,027
(The clock ticks away quickly.)

1609
01:04:51,397 --> 01:04:53,527
(But he's not done organizing his fridge.)

1610
01:04:54,867 --> 01:04:57,067
Close the doors! Just please close the doors.

1611
01:04:58,737 --> 01:05:00,667
Today, I'll become a planner.

1612
01:05:00,667 --> 01:05:01,707
(Will PLAN KUNST be on pause?)

1613
01:05:03,307 --> 01:05:04,367
- No, don't scratch your nose, - Is he contemplating?

1614
01:05:04,367 --> 01:05:05,437
and close the doors.

1615
01:05:05,437 --> 01:05:06,977
- I'm almost done, though. - Yes, just carry on.

1616
01:05:06,977 --> 01:05:08,007
You can keep going.

1617
01:05:08,477 --> 01:05:09,607
What's the next task?

1618
01:05:10,907 --> 01:05:13,617
Oh, lunch? Okay, then.

1619
01:05:15,247 --> 01:05:17,087
I should eat quickly, then.

1620
01:05:17,087 --> 01:05:19,117
- He's making use of the time. - Forty minutes is too long.

1621
01:05:19,387 --> 01:05:21,017
He's taking time from the next one.

1622
01:05:21,427 --> 01:05:22,857
I'll finish lunch in 25 minutes.

1623
01:05:22,857 --> 01:05:23,957
You cut down on mealtime.

1624
01:05:25,157 --> 01:05:27,057
(It's 1:01pm.)

1625
01:05:28,927 --> 01:05:31,337
(He'll keep organizing the fridge for the remaining 14 minutes.)

1626
01:05:31,337 --> 01:05:33,567
(Picking up)

1627
01:05:35,737 --> 01:05:38,077
Organizing the fridge isn't an easy task.

1628
01:05:38,077 --> 01:05:39,777
- It's hard. - It's hard.

1629
01:05:41,707 --> 01:05:44,247
(As he's late, he quickly moves.)

1630
01:05:45,177 --> 01:05:47,577
(So he wouldn't be late for the plan he fixed,)

1631
01:05:47,747 --> 01:05:50,187
(he keeps checking the time.)

1632
01:05:51,687 --> 01:05:52,917
(Pouring out)

1633
01:05:52,917 --> 01:05:54,217
You need to move boldly at a time like this.

1634
01:05:54,487 --> 01:05:56,227
You need to do household chores on a day like that.

1635
01:05:56,687 --> 01:05:57,727
That's right.

1636
01:06:01,157 --> 01:06:02,297
Oh, yes.

1637
01:06:02,297 --> 01:06:03,467
You're good.

1638
01:06:06,467 --> 01:06:07,697
- It's neat, indeed. - And you can take it out...

1639
01:06:07,697 --> 01:06:08,967
right away.

1640
01:06:09,137 --> 01:06:10,437
You're right.

1641
01:06:11,407 --> 01:06:13,477
This is crazy. The fridge looks cool.

1642
01:06:15,007 --> 01:06:16,747
(Satisfied)

1643
01:06:18,817 --> 01:06:20,477
(12:30pm to 1pm, return home and organize the fridge)

1644
01:06:20,477 --> 01:06:23,017
(1:15pm to 1:40pm, lunch)

1645
01:06:23,347 --> 01:06:24,417
Now, then...

1646
01:06:24,417 --> 01:06:26,157
(He prepares lunch right away.)

1647
01:06:26,157 --> 01:06:27,257
What is this?

1648
01:06:31,997 --> 01:06:33,397
(Checking the time)

1649
01:06:33,397 --> 01:06:35,297
Wait. I have to eat within ten minutes.

1650
01:06:35,297 --> 01:06:37,067
(I should make it quickly and eat it.)

1651
01:06:39,037 --> 01:06:40,997
But it must take time to make lunch.

1652
01:06:40,997 --> 01:06:43,237
(Preparing sausages that can be cooked fast...)

1653
01:06:43,567 --> 01:06:47,137
(and fried eggs that's easy to cook)

1654
01:06:48,007 --> 01:06:49,047
All done.

1655
01:06:51,247 --> 01:06:52,577
It'll be perfect if I eat this as well.

1656
01:06:55,617 --> 01:06:58,357
(With instant rice,)

1657
01:06:58,357 --> 01:07:00,657
(he's done preparing lunch.)

1658
01:07:02,827 --> 01:07:04,187
- That's American-style breakfast. - Wait.

1659
01:07:04,187 --> 01:07:06,097
Bread and rice? What kind of combination is that?

1660
01:07:07,257 --> 01:07:09,767
(It's 1:33pm.)

1661
01:07:09,767 --> 01:07:11,997
(He has only 7 minutes to finish his lunch.)

1662
01:07:15,607 --> 01:07:16,837
(He eats bread without hesitation.)

1663
01:07:16,837 --> 01:07:19,507
Having bread and rice at the same time feels new to me.

1664
01:07:19,507 --> 01:07:20,607
It really is unusual.

1665
01:07:20,607 --> 01:07:21,707
Is that so?

1666
01:07:23,747 --> 01:07:26,917
(Nutrient consumption for survival)

1667
01:07:26,917 --> 01:07:27,917
(Taking a glance)

1668
01:07:28,117 --> 01:07:29,787
How many minutes do I have?

1669
01:07:30,487 --> 01:07:32,017
(It's 1:37pm.)

1670
01:07:32,017 --> 01:07:33,017
He has three minutes now.

1671
01:07:33,017 --> 01:07:34,927
- Gosh, you don't have time. - What will he do?

1672
01:07:36,427 --> 01:07:39,097
(Munching)

1673
01:07:39,897 --> 01:07:41,497
He's forcing down food because there's no soup.

1674
01:07:41,797 --> 01:07:44,267
He really eats to survive.

1675
01:07:44,267 --> 01:07:46,137
But I really enjoyed it.

1676
01:07:49,037 --> 01:07:51,037
(It's 1:39pm.)

1677
01:07:52,177 --> 01:07:54,277
- He's having a banana too. - Gosh, it must be so dry.

1678
01:07:54,277 --> 01:07:56,407
(He even has a dessert with 1 minute left.)

1679
01:08:02,617 --> 01:08:05,017
I feel like the alarm is going to drive me crazy.

1680
01:08:05,017 --> 01:08:06,257
- Yes. I'm so stressed. - That's right.

1681
01:08:06,257 --> 01:08:07,417
Even a planner finds it hard.

1682
01:08:08,587 --> 01:08:10,657
(Done with lunch)

1683
01:08:13,557 --> 01:08:16,027
(I'm full.)

1684
01:08:16,027 --> 01:08:17,127
I'm so full.

1685
01:08:18,297 --> 01:08:21,737
(He does the dishes as soon as he's done.)

1686
01:08:22,107 --> 01:08:24,267
Gosh, I forgot the resting time.

1687
01:08:25,507 --> 01:08:27,137
(1pm to 1:40pm, lunch)

1688
01:08:27,137 --> 01:08:28,377
(1:40pm to 1:50pm, rest)

1689
01:08:28,377 --> 01:08:29,707
You had ten minutes to rest?

1690
01:08:29,707 --> 01:08:30,747
I added time to rest.

1691
01:08:30,907 --> 01:08:32,617
You'll need to do the dishes instead of resting.

1692
01:08:32,617 --> 01:08:34,147
(PLAN KUNST even planned a time to rest.)

1693
01:08:34,147 --> 01:08:35,187
Four minutes?

1694
01:08:36,947 --> 01:08:38,357
That kind of helped.

1695
01:08:38,587 --> 01:08:39,687
- Really? - Really?

1696
01:08:39,687 --> 01:08:41,787
Otherwise, I'd have taken five hours to organize the fridge.

1697
01:08:41,887 --> 01:08:42,887
It's true I've never seen you...

1698
01:08:42,887 --> 01:08:43,927
- move that fast. - That's right.

1699
01:08:44,627 --> 01:08:49,067
(He speeds up to meet the time.)

1700
01:08:51,097 --> 01:08:53,697
(I need to finish this in 4 minutes.)

1701
01:08:55,037 --> 01:08:56,467
(I'm going to finish the dishes within the resting time.)

1702
01:08:57,107 --> 01:09:00,137
(1:40pm to 1:50pm, rest)

1703
01:09:01,037 --> 01:09:03,277
(Fortunately, he finished the dishes within time.)

1704
01:09:06,147 --> 01:09:07,217
That's done.

1705
01:09:07,517 --> 01:09:09,117
- I'm done with lunch. - I think this is better.

1706
01:09:09,317 --> 01:09:11,087
It looks like a diligent life.

1707
01:09:11,087 --> 01:09:13,587
Remember how I did that before?

1708
01:09:13,817 --> 01:09:15,827
- Locking up my phone? - I remember.

1709
01:09:16,327 --> 01:09:17,627
(I need to detox.)

1710
01:09:17,957 --> 01:09:19,227
(Don't come out!)

1711
01:09:19,497 --> 01:09:21,597
(He tried to take a break from screen time with determination.)

1712
01:09:21,597 --> 01:09:22,997
(8 hours and 28 minutes)

1713
01:09:22,997 --> 01:09:24,727
(He used his phone for more than eight hours.)

1714
01:09:25,397 --> 01:09:27,837
But now, I only use my phone 3 to 4 hours a day.

1715
01:09:27,837 --> 01:09:28,867
- Really? - Gosh.

1716
01:09:28,867 --> 01:09:30,337
You reduced it a lot.

1717
01:09:30,337 --> 01:09:33,537
Just like that. I'm a really spontaneous person.

1718
01:09:33,837 --> 01:09:37,077
I thought I needed to improve my daily routines.

1719
01:09:38,317 --> 01:09:39,377
Within 30 minutes.

1720
01:09:39,377 --> 01:09:40,377
(He moves on to the next task.)

1721
01:09:40,517 --> 01:09:43,747
(1:50pm to 2:30pm, clean Mabaem's house)

1722
01:09:44,447 --> 01:09:45,787
All right, Mabaem.

1723
01:09:49,357 --> 01:09:52,557
(A corner of his room is filled with things.)

1724
01:09:53,697 --> 01:09:55,397
(A room for vivariums)

1725
01:09:55,727 --> 01:09:56,867
It's gotten bigger.

1726
01:09:57,597 --> 01:09:59,367
- Oh, as in lizard. - Oh, lizard.

1727
01:09:59,367 --> 01:10:00,367
(1:50pm to 2:30pm, clean Mabaem's house)

1728
01:10:01,097 --> 01:10:05,237
(His love for lizards began with Dae Ho.)

1729
01:10:05,437 --> 01:10:06,477
A lizard.

1730
01:10:06,477 --> 01:10:07,607
(He decorated his first vivarium.)

1731
01:10:07,607 --> 01:10:09,277
It's going up!

1732
01:10:09,507 --> 01:10:10,847
Where? Here?

1733
01:10:11,777 --> 01:10:14,477
(His vivariums have been upgraded.)

1734
01:10:14,987 --> 01:10:17,317
This is a carefully planned vivarium.

1735
01:10:17,387 --> 01:10:19,017
- That's so cool. - He's doing it in earnest.

1736
01:10:20,917 --> 01:10:22,627
(The temperature and humidity are carefully taken care of.)

1737
01:10:24,127 --> 01:10:26,257
- They were the ones you had. - Yes, Cono and Coho.

1738
01:10:26,257 --> 01:10:27,827
(Cono and Coho he had before and a baby lizard)

1739
01:10:29,627 --> 01:10:30,667
(Cute)

1740
01:10:30,667 --> 01:10:32,337
Goodness, how cute!

1741
01:10:32,337 --> 01:10:34,637
- This is a new guy. - Oh, it's new?

1742
01:10:35,067 --> 01:10:37,637
They're the same species, but they're male and female.

1743
01:10:37,937 --> 01:10:40,837
They made me think that I raised them well...

1744
01:10:41,177 --> 01:10:42,407
because they spawned a lot.

1745
01:10:42,707 --> 01:10:45,677
So there are three eggs in the incubator.

1746
01:10:45,777 --> 01:10:49,287
I'm maintaining the eggs myself.

1747
01:10:49,287 --> 01:10:51,017
- I'm trying my best. - They have a good relationship.

1748
01:10:51,147 --> 01:10:52,387
So you have five in total.

1749
01:10:52,387 --> 01:10:54,887
There are 5 lizards and 3 eggs, if you count the eggs.

1750
01:10:54,887 --> 01:10:55,887
It's mystical.

1751
01:10:55,887 --> 01:10:57,687
I think he surpassed Dae Ho.

1752
01:10:59,997 --> 01:11:02,827
Dae Ho spread this in his living room.

1753
01:11:03,297 --> 01:11:04,327
That's right.

1754
01:11:04,697 --> 01:11:06,637
What's worse is that that's the same kind of sand.

1755
01:11:06,637 --> 01:11:08,307
He's become a human lizard.

1756
01:11:08,837 --> 01:11:10,607
How did he spread this in the living room?

1757
01:11:11,407 --> 01:11:13,407
Dae Ho, come out for a second.

1758
01:11:15,177 --> 01:11:17,047
(Dae Ho ended up becoming a lizard himself.)

1759
01:11:17,047 --> 01:11:18,577
- They're so cute. - They're bigger guys.

1760
01:11:20,047 --> 01:11:22,647
Dae Ho, I need to clean that part. Can you move?

1761
01:11:23,087 --> 01:11:25,087
I still can't believe he used this on himself.

1762
01:11:26,017 --> 01:11:28,327
I kept getting reminded of that scene.

1763
01:11:28,527 --> 01:11:30,257
- Dae Ho must love it. - Yes.

1764
01:11:30,757 --> 01:11:33,597
(I should spread this for my lizards instead.)

1765
01:11:34,857 --> 01:11:35,897
Mabaem.

1766
01:11:35,897 --> 01:11:37,827
- How adorable. - Look at its eyes.

1767
01:11:38,437 --> 01:11:40,537
(He evenly spreads sand with love.)

1768
01:11:42,467 --> 01:11:43,867
Mabaem, can you move?

1769
01:11:44,207 --> 01:11:45,207
(Jumping)

1770
01:11:45,207 --> 01:11:47,037
- Don't get mad. - It's so cute.

1771
01:11:48,447 --> 01:11:50,577
(Satisfied)

1772
01:11:53,817 --> 01:11:55,587
(He grabs the lower case after he's done cleaning the upper one.)

1773
01:11:55,587 --> 01:11:57,717
Gosh, I should clean this.

1774
01:12:01,627 --> 01:12:03,557
Mabaem. Oh, okay. I'm sorry.

1775
01:12:04,487 --> 01:12:05,997
You always get mad at me during the day.

1776
01:12:07,997 --> 01:12:10,767
(Even though Mabaem is mad, he cleans the house with love.)

1777
01:12:11,937 --> 01:12:13,567
- Look at you. - Oh, my.

1778
01:12:13,737 --> 01:12:15,837
- It's so cute. - I know. It's so adorable.

1779
01:12:15,837 --> 01:12:18,137
You'd be mesmerized when you touch it.

1780
01:12:18,407 --> 01:12:20,537
(Spraying)

1781
01:12:21,077 --> 01:12:22,607
(Providing refreshing water)

1782
01:12:23,347 --> 01:12:24,477
It's so cute!

1783
01:12:24,477 --> 01:12:26,717
- Gosh, it's so cute. - It keeps coming to you.

1784
01:12:26,717 --> 01:12:28,417
- It keeps coming to your side. - It must be in a good mood.

1785
01:12:28,417 --> 01:12:31,147
(While he's focused on his task...)

1786
01:12:31,147 --> 01:12:32,517
Go back in now.

1787
01:12:34,117 --> 01:12:35,827
(Pulling)

1788
01:12:40,157 --> 01:12:42,127
- Gosh, look at that. - Look. A thermometer.

1789
01:12:42,127 --> 01:12:43,297
That's what a pork belly restaurant uses.

1790
01:12:44,297 --> 01:12:45,337
All right.

1791
01:12:46,567 --> 01:12:47,967
You must study a lot to maintain those.

1792
01:12:47,967 --> 01:12:49,067
- You must study. - It's so cute.

1793
01:12:49,067 --> 01:12:50,107
He's really serious about this.

1794
01:12:50,107 --> 01:12:53,177
I've always liked raising things since I was young.

1795
01:12:53,277 --> 01:12:55,277
You need to study a lot to raise lizards.

1796
01:12:55,277 --> 01:12:56,307
That's right.

1797
01:12:57,147 --> 01:12:58,717
(Wiping every single leaf)

1798
01:12:58,717 --> 01:13:00,547
- So much effort. - He's putting in so much effort.

1799
01:13:00,977 --> 01:13:02,847
If you don't clean them,

1800
01:13:03,187 --> 01:13:06,017
they create toxic gases.

1801
01:13:07,417 --> 01:13:08,787
Go back in now.

1802
01:13:09,687 --> 01:13:10,887
Why wouldn't you go in?

1803
01:13:10,887 --> 01:13:12,057
(Crawling around as they are excited by the new sand)

1804
01:13:12,727 --> 01:13:14,127
(Feeling proud)

1805
01:13:14,657 --> 01:13:16,127
(Satisfied with a cleaner house)

1806
01:13:16,127 --> 01:13:17,967
- It's so cute! - Look at you.

1807
01:13:19,797 --> 01:13:20,897
And then...

1808
01:13:21,467 --> 01:13:22,937
(Opening)

1809
01:13:22,937 --> 01:13:25,877
These eggs... It's about time they hatched.

1810
01:13:27,577 --> 01:13:28,607
Are you hatching them?

1811
01:13:28,607 --> 01:13:30,547
I didn't expect this either.

1812
01:13:30,547 --> 01:13:32,317
They must have a really good relationship.

1813
01:13:32,577 --> 01:13:33,877
September 14.

1814
01:13:34,017 --> 01:13:36,447
These are viable eggs for sure.

1815
01:13:37,017 --> 01:13:38,487
It's about time they hatched.

1816
01:13:38,487 --> 01:13:40,487
He lives alone there, so how come that place has such good energy?

1817
01:13:40,487 --> 01:13:42,257
I'm not sure about this one, though.

1818
01:13:43,057 --> 01:13:45,797
(I hope they hatch soon.)

1819
01:13:51,127 --> 01:13:52,137
(Knocking)

1820
01:13:52,137 --> 01:13:53,137
- What? - What?

1821
01:13:54,837 --> 01:13:56,667
(Who is it?)

1822
01:13:57,307 --> 01:13:58,292
Yes? Okay.

1823
01:13:58,317 --> 01:14:00,378
(Someone is visiting him when he doesn't have much time?)

1824
01:14:01,707 --> 01:14:02,837
Gosh.

1825
01:14:03,637 --> 01:14:04,677
What is it?

1826
01:14:06,277 --> 01:14:07,307
What's that?

1827
01:14:08,047 --> 01:14:09,577
- Is it a tree? - A plant must've been delivered.

1828
01:14:09,677 --> 01:14:11,747
That wasn't part of my plan.

1829
01:14:12,787 --> 01:14:15,317
(Here's an unexpected variable.)

1830
01:14:16,117 --> 01:14:17,487
This had to come now?

1831
01:14:17,487 --> 01:14:19,627
(The tree had to be delivered now, of all times?)

1832
01:14:21,697 --> 01:14:24,257
(I still have more to clean.)

1833
01:14:25,527 --> 01:14:27,627
- This has to come now? - His tasks are getting backed up.

1834
01:14:28,537 --> 01:14:31,367
What do you do in a case like this?

1835
01:14:31,367 --> 01:14:33,107
For instance? What would you do, KEY or Na Rae?

1836
01:14:33,107 --> 01:14:34,307
- If this happens, - Yes.

1837
01:14:34,307 --> 01:14:36,437
I'd acknowledge the tree,

1838
01:14:36,437 --> 01:14:38,477
then I'd go back to the previous task and wrap it up.

1839
01:14:38,477 --> 01:14:41,477
Then I'd squeeze that in somewhere in the plan.

1840
01:14:44,477 --> 01:14:45,547
He's getting to it right away.

1841
01:14:45,547 --> 01:14:47,587
- He does it right away. - We get it done right away.

1842
01:14:48,887 --> 01:14:52,587
That's the tree I ordered 2 or 3 days ago.

1843
01:14:52,587 --> 01:14:54,697
I was really flustered...

1844
01:14:54,697 --> 01:14:56,727
because the plan will go off track.

1845
01:14:57,327 --> 01:14:59,067
"But I'm a planner."

1846
01:14:59,667 --> 01:15:01,567
"This shouldn't happen." That's what I thought.

1847
01:15:03,297 --> 01:15:04,837
Let's put this here for now.

1848
01:15:11,347 --> 01:15:12,947
Wait. This looks strange.

1849
01:15:12,947 --> 01:15:14,817
Gosh, he shouldn't be fixated on that.

1850
01:15:14,817 --> 01:15:16,447
- What is that? - It's over if he becomes fixated.

1851
01:15:16,447 --> 01:15:17,777
I didn't like where it was.

1852
01:15:17,847 --> 01:15:20,317
It's not that aesthetically pleasing.

1853
01:15:23,187 --> 01:15:25,527
Wait a minute. I'm pressed for time.

1854
01:15:25,757 --> 01:15:27,997
- No, you have to clean. - Why doesn't it suit the room?

1855
01:15:28,557 --> 01:15:30,597
(It's less than 5 minutes before the cleaning time is over.)

1856
01:15:31,967 --> 01:15:33,027
Over here?

1857
01:15:33,027 --> 01:15:35,497
(He quickly moves the pot.)

1858
01:15:39,367 --> 01:15:41,307
Gosh, it looks somewhat messy.

1859
01:15:42,507 --> 01:15:43,577
If so...

1860
01:15:44,707 --> 01:15:47,277
- He's fixated on that. - Oh, no.

1861
01:15:47,277 --> 01:15:48,347
(He's replacing the pot when he must be cleaning the lizards' houses.)

1862
01:15:48,477 --> 01:15:49,517
- Gosh. - What do we do?

1863
01:15:51,387 --> 01:15:52,647
I should get back to my original task.

1864
01:15:52,887 --> 01:15:54,387
That's right. Goodness.

1865
01:15:55,687 --> 01:15:57,927
(He quickly gets back to the lizard house cleaning.)

1866
01:15:59,287 --> 01:16:00,957
(Rushing)

1867
01:16:04,227 --> 01:16:05,297
No!

1868
01:16:07,067 --> 01:16:08,337
- Gosh, that's bad. - Wait a minute.

1869
01:16:08,337 --> 01:16:09,367
- Gosh, I'm so stressed. - I thought it was my phone...

1870
01:16:09,367 --> 01:16:10,497
- that was ringing. - I'm stressed now.

1871
01:16:10,497 --> 01:16:11,507
It's 2pm.

1872
01:16:11,507 --> 01:16:13,607
What was I supposed to do now?

1873
01:16:14,567 --> 01:16:15,777
(Rushing)

1874
01:16:15,777 --> 01:16:17,077
He's going back to the fridge to check?

1875
01:16:17,077 --> 01:16:18,377
Still, you did your best.

1876
01:16:20,877 --> 01:16:23,447
Oh, cleaning the cats' blankets.

1877
01:16:23,447 --> 01:16:24,647
Cleaning the cats' blankets?

1878
01:16:24,647 --> 01:16:25,687
Cats?

1879
01:16:26,947 --> 01:16:30,357
(I need to quickly clean the cats' blanket.)

1880
01:16:31,827 --> 01:16:33,987
(He quickly wraps up cleaning the lizards' house for now.)

1881
01:16:33,987 --> 01:16:35,157
I'm being rushed now.

1882
01:16:36,357 --> 01:16:38,627
(I'm so busy.)

1883
01:16:39,227 --> 01:16:41,097
I need to clean this room too.

1884
01:16:41,497 --> 01:16:43,397
(Tasks have piled up in no time.)

1885
01:16:43,767 --> 01:16:44,937
(2:30pm to 3pm, clean the cats' blanket and litter boxes)

1886
01:16:44,937 --> 01:16:46,007
(The clock is ticking.)

1887
01:16:46,507 --> 01:16:48,177
- You should wrap that up first. - That's right.

1888
01:16:49,107 --> 01:16:50,137
First of all,

1889
01:16:50,877 --> 01:16:53,207
I'll clean the blankets quickly.

1890
01:16:53,947 --> 01:16:56,377
I'll save ten minutes from there...

1891
01:16:56,377 --> 01:16:58,187
and use that time to clean up this room.

1892
01:16:58,547 --> 01:17:00,517
(That sounds okay.)

1893
01:17:01,187 --> 01:17:02,187
Okay.

1894
01:17:02,657 --> 01:17:04,087
(Get to it right away.)

1895
01:17:04,087 --> 01:17:05,157
Then...

1896
01:17:07,057 --> 01:17:09,827
(It's 2:34pm.)

1897
01:17:10,697 --> 01:17:13,667
- That song is driving me crazy. - That song... Seriously.

1898
01:17:15,237 --> 01:17:16,297
(2:30pm to 3pm, clean the cats' blanket and litter boxes)

1899
01:17:16,297 --> 01:17:17,797
What? How do I undo this?

1900
01:17:18,137 --> 01:17:19,307
- The cat tower? - Yes.

1901
01:17:20,637 --> 01:17:21,907
What about the blankets?

1902
01:17:21,907 --> 01:17:24,777
So while I was cleaning the blankets,

1903
01:17:24,977 --> 01:17:26,477
I was reminded of that.

1904
01:17:26,477 --> 01:17:27,607
Right. Well done.

1905
01:17:29,617 --> 01:17:32,717
(He's given up his plan.)

1906
01:17:32,717 --> 01:17:34,447
This must be waterproof.

1907
01:17:34,447 --> 01:17:36,587
- This wasn't part of his plan. - Yes.

1908
01:17:36,787 --> 01:17:38,487
This song is driving me crazy.

1909
01:17:38,527 --> 01:17:40,257
I'll be reminded of it when I go back home.

1910
01:17:41,187 --> 01:17:42,757
- You should throw them. - That's good.

1911
01:17:43,027 --> 01:17:44,057
(Throwing)

1912
01:17:44,057 --> 01:17:46,167
- Throw them. - Did you watch this in advance?

1913
01:17:46,167 --> 01:17:47,267
(Fascinated)

1914
01:17:47,897 --> 01:17:48,897
Spontaneous people just throw them.

1915
01:17:48,897 --> 01:17:50,267
It feels like you watched it in advance.

1916
01:17:50,297 --> 01:17:51,297
(Throwing)

1917
01:17:52,107 --> 01:17:53,867
- He'll reposition them? - Reposition furniture.

1918
01:17:54,567 --> 01:17:55,877
Let's try turning it.

1919
01:17:55,877 --> 01:17:57,477
(I must reposition the sofa, if nothing else.)

1920
01:17:58,007 --> 01:18:00,707
It was my first time moving that sofa since I moved.

1921
01:18:00,707 --> 01:18:01,717
- I see. - I see.

1922
01:18:01,877 --> 01:18:03,117
So that's how it looks.

1923
01:18:03,817 --> 01:18:04,917
Hey!

1924
01:18:05,447 --> 01:18:07,117
(Crawling in)

1925
01:18:07,487 --> 01:18:09,217
Why would you go in there? Hey, come out.

1926
01:18:11,487 --> 01:18:13,087
- Right, cats ought to... - How cute!

1927
01:18:13,087 --> 01:18:14,227
- You punk. - It looks like the cat...

1928
01:18:14,227 --> 01:18:15,297
from "Puss in Boots."

1929
01:18:15,297 --> 01:18:16,997
- Look at you. - It's like the cat from "Shrek."

1930
01:18:16,997 --> 01:18:20,027
- I know. From "Puss in Boots." - It's so cute.

1931
01:18:20,427 --> 01:18:22,397
You're the variable.

1932
01:18:23,567 --> 01:18:24,967
- Look at you. - How cute.

1933
01:18:24,967 --> 01:18:26,037
- It's so cute. How can you... - How cute.

1934
01:18:26,037 --> 01:18:27,967
- not take a photo of that? - Tell me about it.

1935
01:18:29,637 --> 01:18:30,637
Siru.

1936
01:18:31,007 --> 01:18:33,477
(In the end, he had to pause his plan again.)

1937
01:18:33,477 --> 01:18:35,117
You can spend 30 minutes like that.

1938
01:18:35,117 --> 01:18:36,117
I know.

1939
01:18:37,317 --> 01:18:38,347
I need to turn it.

1940
01:18:39,647 --> 01:18:41,787
(He comes to his senses again and repositions the sofa.)

1941
01:18:42,957 --> 01:18:47,587
(He brings a vacuum cleaner in the middle of repositioning.)

1942
01:18:49,457 --> 01:18:52,197
(He moves the sofa again.)

1943
01:18:53,067 --> 01:18:54,567
(Then he suddenly cleans his dressing room.)

1944
01:18:54,567 --> 01:18:55,697
Siru, come here.

1945
01:18:59,237 --> 01:19:00,367
(Now, he doesn't care if the alarm rings or not.)

1946
01:19:00,367 --> 01:19:02,337
It's ringing again. What is it this time?

1947
01:19:03,207 --> 01:19:04,277
(Forget the plan. It's all messed up.)

1948
01:19:04,277 --> 01:19:05,847
Gosh, that sound. Seriously...

1949
01:19:05,847 --> 01:19:07,407
- I'm so stressed. - I'm so stressed too.

1950
01:19:09,417 --> 01:19:10,417
The laundry is done.

1951
01:19:10,917 --> 01:19:12,817
- The blankets? - The blankets are done.

1952
01:19:12,817 --> 01:19:14,857
- Why are you laughing? - I got hit with reality a bit.

1953
01:19:14,857 --> 01:19:15,857
Oh, no.

1954
01:19:16,557 --> 01:19:17,557
Goodness.

1955
01:19:18,157 --> 01:19:21,057
(Laughing)

1956
01:19:22,097 --> 01:19:23,757
He's losing it.

1957
01:19:26,067 --> 01:19:27,627
(The alarm became his work song.)

1958
01:19:27,827 --> 01:19:28,867
The cat left.

1959
01:19:28,867 --> 01:19:30,667
(Everything is a mess.)

1960
01:19:33,367 --> 01:19:34,837
It's already 5:30pm?

1961
01:19:38,007 --> 01:19:39,907
- Why do you look so worn out? - Your eyes...

1962
01:19:39,907 --> 01:19:40,907
- The dark circles... - I...

1963
01:19:40,907 --> 01:19:42,377
- He lost weight. - I lost weight.

1964
01:19:42,577 --> 01:19:44,077
This is why it's dangerous to be a planner.

1965
01:19:44,077 --> 01:19:45,687
- What are you talking about? - What are you talking about?

1966
01:19:49,017 --> 01:19:50,387
It's nice to rest.

1967
01:19:50,987 --> 01:19:52,757
(But the house is a mess.)

1968
01:19:52,757 --> 01:19:53,757
Everything's brought out.

1969
01:19:57,897 --> 01:19:59,197
You're watching your own clip.

1970
01:19:59,927 --> 01:20:00,967
No, I'm not.

1971
01:20:01,897 --> 01:20:03,237
Is that a first birthday party?

1972
01:20:03,267 --> 01:20:04,967
He's doing doljabi.

1973
01:20:05,737 --> 01:20:06,937
Doljabi?

1974
01:20:08,337 --> 01:20:09,377
No.

1975
01:20:09,377 --> 01:20:10,807
(The alarm went off while watching his doljabi clip.)

1976
01:20:11,507 --> 01:20:13,377
(Getting up)

1977
01:20:14,077 --> 01:20:15,317
What's he doing now?

1978
01:20:15,917 --> 01:20:17,217
He's kicking everything around now.

1979
01:20:17,377 --> 01:20:18,717
I think your anger is building up.

1980
01:20:18,717 --> 01:20:20,287
You need to go see Chung Jae.

1981
01:20:20,547 --> 01:20:22,357
- Call me next time you see him. - Okay.

1982
01:20:23,317 --> 01:20:24,787
Your anger disappears when you meet him.

1983
01:20:26,787 --> 01:20:29,057
(Taking the planner he stuck on the fridge)

1984
01:20:29,197 --> 01:20:30,827
- He's leaving just like that? - That's right.

1985
01:20:31,127 --> 01:20:32,497
He leaves at exactly 6pm.

1986
01:20:32,497 --> 01:20:34,067
- Oh, to the studio. - Oh, to the studio.

1987
01:20:34,397 --> 01:20:35,837
This is important.

1988
01:20:35,967 --> 01:20:38,537
I thought I needed to finish working on the songs...

1989
01:20:38,537 --> 01:20:40,767
that were 70 to 80 percent done.

1990
01:20:41,437 --> 01:20:44,707
Actually, I spent the day like that because of this.

1991
01:20:44,707 --> 01:20:45,907
- Because you need to... - Of course.

1992
01:20:45,907 --> 01:20:47,147
- spend more time on your music. - To make good music...

1993
01:20:48,317 --> 01:20:49,847
(He heads to the studio on his bike.)

1994
01:20:51,147 --> 01:20:52,987
(Worn out)

1995
01:20:52,987 --> 01:20:54,587
- What's wrong with your face? - Are you sure you're okay?

1996
01:20:54,587 --> 01:20:55,957
- Why are your under-eyes so dark? - The reverse triangle zone...

1997
01:20:55,957 --> 01:20:57,717
stands out especially today.

1998
01:20:57,717 --> 01:20:58,927
You look so...

1999
01:20:58,927 --> 01:21:00,527
I did too much work that day.

2000
01:21:01,657 --> 01:21:02,927
(Laughing)

2001
01:21:04,227 --> 01:21:05,467
(Speeding on the bicycle path)

2002
01:21:05,467 --> 01:21:08,897
It was the evening rush hour. It was right at that time.

2003
01:21:09,397 --> 01:21:10,497
Because there's traffic.

2004
01:21:11,037 --> 01:21:12,937
I wondered how I could get there within 30 minutes.

2005
01:21:13,467 --> 01:21:16,207
I could only do that by bike, if not by teleportation.

2006
01:21:17,537 --> 01:21:19,577
Bicycles are the best there.

2007
01:21:19,577 --> 01:21:21,447
- It's way faster. - What is this?

2008
01:21:21,447 --> 01:21:22,677
(Do not ride bicycles here.)

2009
01:21:22,677 --> 01:21:24,517
- You have to push it. Right. - That's right.

2010
01:21:24,517 --> 01:21:26,447
- You have to push it on a bridge. - You have to push it on a bridge.

2011
01:21:27,347 --> 01:21:29,617
(I've been walking for quite some time.)

2012
01:21:29,617 --> 01:21:31,587
(I might be late at this rate.)

2013
01:21:32,557 --> 01:21:35,127
It takes exactly 30 minutes to get there, right?

2014
01:21:39,097 --> 01:21:40,097
Gosh, the alarm.

2015
01:21:40,097 --> 01:21:42,967
(The alarm goes off as soon as he reaches.)

2016
01:21:43,597 --> 01:21:45,837
(6pm to 6:30pm, going to the studio)

2017
01:21:46,537 --> 01:21:47,537
I'm here.

2018
01:21:48,337 --> 01:21:49,377
That's amazing.

2019
01:21:49,707 --> 01:21:51,647
I feel like he needs a nap now.

2020
01:21:51,647 --> 01:21:53,207
No, he should get an IV drip.

2021
01:21:53,977 --> 01:21:54,977
Really?

2022
01:21:56,717 --> 01:21:58,317
(Setting something up instead of an IV drip)

2023
01:21:59,987 --> 01:22:03,387
I should blend these two.

2024
01:22:06,027 --> 01:22:07,287
Gosh, what is that?

2025
01:22:08,697 --> 01:22:09,757
A coffee machine.

2026
01:22:17,037 --> 01:22:18,567
- Its design is really nice too. - Yes.

2027
01:22:20,507 --> 01:22:21,877
If I remove this...

2028
01:22:23,777 --> 01:22:26,147
(Coffee transfusion that will ease his fatigue)

2029
01:22:27,207 --> 01:22:28,647
(Satisfied)

2030
01:22:30,277 --> 01:22:32,317
(He heads straight to the studio after recharging his energy.)

2031
01:22:34,887 --> 01:22:37,357
(Let me see.)

2032
01:22:38,557 --> 01:22:39,627
Let's start working now.

2033
01:22:40,057 --> 01:22:41,357
- You should turn that off! - Darn it.

2034
01:22:41,357 --> 01:22:42,497
Just turn it off now.

2035
01:22:43,757 --> 01:22:45,427
(The alarm rang the whole day.)

2036
01:22:45,427 --> 01:22:47,627
- It's like a Japanese horror movie. - Exactly.

2037
01:22:49,297 --> 01:22:52,167
I look like an artist after a long time with dark circles.

2038
01:22:54,407 --> 01:22:56,077
(9:30pm, finish working on the song)

2039
01:22:56,077 --> 01:22:58,137
(He'll focus on work until 9:30pm.)

2040
01:22:58,137 --> 01:22:59,247
You'll start right away?

2041
01:22:59,247 --> 01:23:01,717
- Without resting? - I didn't want to go off schedule.

2042
01:23:05,017 --> 01:23:06,917
(Even though he's tired because he moved around all day...)

2043
01:23:06,917 --> 01:23:08,587
If I didn't get to work, all the things I did during the day...

2044
01:23:08,587 --> 01:23:09,617
would be pointless.

2045
01:23:09,617 --> 01:23:10,687
- True. - That's right.

2046
01:23:10,687 --> 01:23:12,857
You did all that so you could focus on work.

2047
01:23:12,857 --> 01:23:15,327
(His ultimate goal is to make nice songs.)

2048
01:23:18,497 --> 01:23:20,667
(Getting up at the sound of the doorbell)

2049
01:23:21,997 --> 01:23:23,037
It came so fast.

2050
01:23:26,367 --> 01:23:27,407
You delivered in?

2051
01:23:27,507 --> 01:23:29,177
(He ordered food before he started working.)

2052
01:23:31,037 --> 01:23:32,277
(Flopping)

2053
01:23:36,917 --> 01:23:38,017
You're not eating it?

2054
01:23:38,947 --> 01:23:40,017
If I eat it...

2055
01:23:40,547 --> 01:23:42,187
- You lose the flow. - Yes, I lose the flow.

2056
01:23:43,887 --> 01:23:45,627
If you started working, then you should continue.

2057
01:23:48,727 --> 01:23:51,827
(Stacking each note carefully)

2058
01:23:53,267 --> 01:23:54,297
Nice.

2059
01:23:54,997 --> 01:23:56,167
He's so cool.

2060
01:23:57,367 --> 01:23:58,437
It must've gone cold.

2061
01:24:03,837 --> 01:24:05,147
(Grabbing)

2062
01:24:06,307 --> 01:24:07,377
- What's that? Gosh! - What?

2063
01:24:10,317 --> 01:24:11,817
- What is that? - What's that?

2064
01:24:12,087 --> 01:24:13,247
Is that a skein or something?

2065
01:24:16,557 --> 01:24:18,857
(Even if he drops his food,)

2066
01:24:18,857 --> 01:24:21,557
(he doesn't miss the beat.)

2067
01:24:21,957 --> 01:24:23,497
He looks like a producer who lives a productive life.

2068
01:24:23,497 --> 01:24:25,027
You can multitask though.

2069
01:24:25,767 --> 01:24:27,967
(To me, even a spoon...)

2070
01:24:27,967 --> 01:24:30,297
(acts as a metronome.)

2071
01:24:32,907 --> 01:24:34,707
(Sparkling)

2072
01:24:36,507 --> 01:24:39,647
(He savors music instead of food.)

2073
01:24:39,647 --> 01:24:40,747
Goodness.

2074
01:24:42,177 --> 01:24:44,147
- That's him swallowing his food. - He's bobbing like this.

2075
01:24:44,147 --> 01:24:45,847
- Is that so? - He's swallowing noodles.

2076
01:24:45,847 --> 01:24:46,947
Because it's not good?

2077
01:24:46,947 --> 01:24:49,817
- It looks like his stomach is full. - His eyes and his head bobbing...

2078
01:24:52,127 --> 01:24:53,127
It's all done.

2079
01:24:53,557 --> 01:24:55,527
Did you complete a song that day?

2080
01:24:56,027 --> 01:24:57,597
I finished three that day.

2081
01:25:00,027 --> 01:25:01,167
You're a super Type J.

2082
01:25:01,467 --> 01:25:04,667
I started working on those songs half a year ago.

2083
01:25:04,667 --> 01:25:05,837
But I got to finish them only now.

2084
01:25:05,837 --> 01:25:07,337
(He finally finished the songs that were pending for half a year.)

2085
01:25:07,637 --> 01:25:11,277
I think it helped improve my concentration.

2086
01:25:11,437 --> 01:25:14,007
There were about ten chapters today,

2087
01:25:14,247 --> 01:25:15,317
You were really busy.

2088
01:25:15,447 --> 01:25:18,317
so I thought it was too much.

2089
01:25:18,377 --> 01:25:21,857
It felt like how I did when I first encountered multi-grain rice.

2090
01:25:22,387 --> 01:25:24,217
I know it's not delicious at all,

2091
01:25:24,217 --> 01:25:26,087
(Even planning)

2092
01:25:26,087 --> 01:25:27,257
but it's good for yourself.

2093
01:25:27,687 --> 01:25:29,857
If I divide my day into two chapters,

2094
01:25:29,857 --> 01:25:31,457
which will be working out and going to the studio,

2095
01:25:31,727 --> 01:25:34,567
that's be a big improvement for me.

2096
01:25:36,567 --> 01:25:37,737
I wrapped up the songs.

2097
01:25:38,167 --> 01:25:39,367
You must've felt proud of yourself.

2098
01:25:39,607 --> 01:25:41,407
I'm a perfect planner.

2099
01:25:42,207 --> 01:25:43,737
You did all those, in any case.

2100
01:25:43,877 --> 01:25:45,077
- I did all of them. - You did.

2101
01:25:45,277 --> 01:25:47,307
Have you been drawing up a plan after that?

2102
01:25:47,307 --> 01:25:49,247
I don't set time for anything.

2103
01:25:49,247 --> 01:25:51,917
I'd think, "I'll go to the studio in the evening."

2104
01:25:51,917 --> 01:25:53,417
- Right. - But I've changed it...

2105
01:25:53,487 --> 01:25:55,087
to going to the studio by 7pm.

2106
01:25:55,087 --> 01:25:56,557
- Or by 8pm. - It's not a rough plan anymore.

2107
01:25:56,557 --> 01:25:58,157
No. My plan got more detailed.

2108
01:25:58,887 --> 01:25:59,927
That's awesome.

2109
01:25:59,927 --> 01:26:01,457
But his house is a mess.

2110
01:26:02,827 --> 01:26:05,097
- You need to plan cleaning again. - That's right.

2111
01:26:08,737 --> 01:26:10,797
(I'm going to enjoy the three completed songs for now.)

2112
01:26:13,067 --> 01:26:15,737
- "A Plan Man." - "A Plan Man."

2113
01:26:16,077 --> 01:26:17,577
(Next story)

2114
01:26:18,207 --> 01:26:19,247
(Hyun Moo, can you beat me?)

2115
01:26:20,077 --> 01:26:22,277
(The history of Moo-Ku started with Sung Hwan's provocation.)

2116
01:26:22,277 --> 01:26:23,747
Let's run 100m in front of the MBC building.

2117
01:26:24,277 --> 01:26:26,087
(They had a barefoot race against each other.)

2118
01:26:26,887 --> 01:26:27,917
(But Hyun Moo easily beat him.)

2119
01:26:28,557 --> 01:26:29,887
(Let's have a proper field day.)

2120
01:26:30,217 --> 01:26:31,887
(Let's do it.)

2121
01:26:31,887 --> 01:26:32,927
(What about prizes?)

2122
01:26:33,557 --> 01:26:35,127
(I prepared them all.)

2123
01:26:36,057 --> 01:26:37,257
(Then I'll take MINHO.)

2124
01:26:38,667 --> 01:26:39,997
(The long-awaited...)

2125
01:26:40,397 --> 01:26:41,937
(The 1st Innocent Moo-Ku Fall Field Day)

2126
01:26:41,937 --> 01:26:42,937
(The field day has started.)

2127
01:26:43,437 --> 01:26:46,437
(They prepared the field day themselves.)

2128
01:26:47,267 --> 01:26:48,737
(Now that we're done preparing, let's have fun!)

2129
01:26:49,437 --> 01:26:51,607
(This is exciting!)

2130
01:26:52,107 --> 01:26:53,747
(Why isn't everyone coming?)

2131
01:26:54,607 --> 01:26:56,377
(A group of people in white appears from afar.)

2132
01:26:56,377 --> 01:26:57,377
They're dancing.

2133
01:26:57,377 --> 01:26:58,517
(We're here for the field day.)

2134
01:26:58,717 --> 01:27:01,047
(Why did you pick a day like this?)

2135
01:27:01,047 --> 01:27:02,287
What are they doing there?

2136
01:27:02,287 --> 01:27:04,517
(A bunch of beloved family members shows up.)

2137
01:27:05,217 --> 01:27:06,527
(Welcome)

2138
01:27:07,327 --> 01:27:10,257
(Kang Yong appeared with his secret weapon.)

2139
01:27:11,327 --> 01:27:13,997
(Splendid prizes that the CEO of MBC send himself)

2140
01:27:14,827 --> 01:27:15,837
(I must win them.)

2141
01:27:16,537 --> 01:27:18,297
(The first crew, Team Chunk)

2142
01:27:19,567 --> 01:27:21,237
(I'll beat them with my sheer strength.)

2143
01:27:22,007 --> 01:27:23,707
(I've been waiting for this day.)

2144
01:27:23,707 --> 01:27:24,707
(Heave-ho)

2145
01:27:24,977 --> 01:27:26,277
(Helpless)

2146
01:27:26,807 --> 01:27:27,907
(The chunk roars.)

2147
01:27:28,477 --> 01:27:30,917
What are you doing? Are you a woodpecker or something?

2148
01:27:31,547 --> 01:27:34,817
(The second crew, Team Weirdos)

2149
01:27:34,817 --> 01:27:35,817
(Immersed)

2150
01:27:36,417 --> 01:27:37,887
(They've become a lot closer,)

2151
01:27:38,687 --> 01:27:40,457
(and it makes them laugh.)

2152
01:27:41,227 --> 01:27:42,797
(The third crew, Team Go-getters)

2153
01:27:43,557 --> 01:27:45,297
(The main go-getter, MINHO)

2154
01:27:45,297 --> 01:27:46,467
(And Ji Hyeon is next to him?)

2155
01:27:47,167 --> 01:27:48,597
(Neck and neck)

2156
01:27:48,897 --> 01:27:50,967
(I'll fight with guts and grit.)

2157
01:27:51,667 --> 01:27:54,937
(Victory and defeat hang by a thread.)

2158
01:27:55,537 --> 01:27:57,077
(We are just as fearless.)

2159
01:27:57,577 --> 01:27:59,077
(Getting interesting)

2160
01:27:59,707 --> 01:28:01,207
(The go-getter finds it unfair.)

2161
01:28:01,947 --> 01:28:03,517
(MINHO, are you ticked off?)

2162
01:28:03,517 --> 01:28:05,017
(MINHO, are you hurt?)

2163
01:28:05,547 --> 01:28:07,647
(Filled with desire to win)

2164
01:28:08,387 --> 01:28:10,257
(Let's face off for real.)

2165
01:28:10,887 --> 01:28:13,527
(I can't let them take even a single point.)

2166
01:28:14,157 --> 01:28:16,457
(A strong determination to win)

2167
01:28:17,057 --> 01:28:18,627
(I can do this.)

2168
01:28:19,267 --> 01:28:20,897
(We don't stop.)

2169
01:28:21,827 --> 01:28:23,437
(But is this right?)

2170
01:28:23,767 --> 01:28:25,097
Let's fight with sincerity.

2171
01:28:26,007 --> 01:28:28,607
(It won't stop raining.)

2172
01:28:30,177 --> 01:28:33,477
(The match won't end either.)

2173
01:28:33,477 --> 01:28:35,147
Everyone's lips are blue.

2174
01:28:35,147 --> 01:28:36,517
- Please... - Lower.

2175
01:28:37,247 --> 01:28:38,687
(We might all die at this rate.)

2176
01:28:38,942 --> 01:28:40,112
Can we just end it?

2177
01:28:40,137 --> 01:28:42,898
(Never Ending Innocent Moo-Ku Field Day)
